00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:30.130
AMY BANZAERT: So last week
was the intro class and then

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.060
the human power lab.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.160
Today, I just told
you what we're doing.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.380
On Friday, we'll have
the energy storage lab,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.620
and then next week
into lighting.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:44.690
And in two weeks is Quiz 1, just
so you have it in your minds.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.650
So the Muddy Cards.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.900
We're going to do
this one at the end.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.040
So there were some
questions on the estimation.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.220
So one was what if I have no
reference point for estimation?

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.910
For example, I have no idea
what a D cell is, basically.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:03.730
So then, you're stuck.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.370
Because it's really
hard, if I ask

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.720
you to estimate the power
in a glob, and you're like,

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.153
what's the glob, Amy?

00:01:12.153 --> 00:01:14.320
And I'm not telling you,
because it's an estimation,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.050
you're stuck.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.720
And so if I literally say
glob, and you're like,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.860
I have no idea what a
glob is, then you're done,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.200
and you have to
reference some of manual.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.980
But if you know D
cell is a battery,

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.930
then the best practice
is to say, OK,

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:31.630
am I familiar with
any battery, say

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:34.970
a double A or a car battery
or a 9-volt or something?

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.490
And say I'm going to--

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.830
just write an explicit
assumption in your estimation.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.650
I'm assuming a D
cell is a 9-volt,

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.110
or a D cell is a car
battery, or whatever

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.450
assumption you make that
brings you to something

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.590
you're familiar with.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.780
And that way, when
you do the estimation,

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.020
and if you do it
clearly, then when

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.360
you have time to
check that assumption,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.730
you can then very quickly
revise your estimate

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.638
based on real-world data.

00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:01.930
And that way, if it's something
you're turning into me,

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.090
or it's something you're
using in the real world,

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.740
you still get to do something.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:09.639
And maybe you'll
have less confidence

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:10.789
in that answer at the end.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:12.997
So maybe you're going to be
off by a couple of orders

00:02:12.997 --> 00:02:14.530
of magnitude rather than one.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.962
But at least you've
done something

00:02:16.962 --> 00:02:18.670
rather than just sat
there looking bored.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.010
But if I say glob, and
you literally are like,

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.890
I have no idea what
you're talking about,

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:27.020
then yeah, that's
a tricky situation.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.300
But hopefully, that
doesn't come up too often.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:29.800
Yeah, Kirk?

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.890
AUDIENCE: So [INAUDIBLE] do
you want us to kind of pull

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.010
a number out of thin air for
something like how much energy,

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.200
like solar-thermal system--

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.138
AMY BANZAERT: So that wasn't
an explicit estimation.

00:02:43.138 --> 00:02:43.680
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.472
AMY BANZAERT: So that
would be a case where

00:02:45.472 --> 00:02:48.390
you should be googling
and documenting

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:54.030
where you get your numbers but,
absolutely, looking stuff up.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.760
Generally, I will say
estimate, if I mean estimate.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.370
But if you're
unclear, always ask.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.580
I'm not 100% responding to
email quickly, but I try to.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.328
And if you don't hear
from me in 24 hours,

00:03:08.328 --> 00:03:09.870
just send another
email, and be like,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.630
seriously, give me the answer.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.000
I get a lot of emails per
day, as I'm sure we all do,

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.830
and sometimes it hurts.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.070
If you put an explicit D-Lab
Energy in the subject line,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:24.060
that will probably improve my
ability to respond quickly.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.730
Because I won't necessarily
recognize your email,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.170
but I will see that a subject.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:31.050
So yeah, but I my intention
is to respond quickly,

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.840
but I got people like emailing
me at 1:00 in the morning,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.372
needing a response
by the next morning.

00:03:36.372 --> 00:03:38.580
And that is hard for me, if
I've already gone to bed.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.170
Sometimes, I haven't,
but if I have,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.600
I usually don't get
to email until midday.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.230
So that's just a warning that,
unfortunately, or fortunately,

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.990
I don't keep 2:00
AM hours anymore.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.960
Usually, I try not to.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.930
Another one was a situation
where we could actually use

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.470
estimates in the real world.

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.830
And I hope that will become
clear as the weeks go on.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.930
But for example, your
community partner

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:09.090
may well say let's put
a wind turbine here.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.100
Could we do wind power here?

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.150
And literally, that
will be the question.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:18.180
And your choice is to say,
I have no idea or yes or no.

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.242
Or give me 10 minutes,
let me do an estimation,

00:04:20.242 --> 00:04:22.200
and then you can give a
much more useful answer

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.470
than any of those.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.780
And so you could
very quickly say,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.390
well, talk to me
about wind speed,

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.000
and we'll talk about how to
estimate wind power later

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.450
on in the semester.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:34.260
But so how much of
the branch is moving?

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.960
Are you falling down
because of the wind,

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.660
or is it just a little
breeze occasionally?

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.482
And get an estimate
of the wind speed,

00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:43.440
and then you can run
through a calculation that

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.960
says, yes or no, you
could power a light bulb

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:46.860
or whatever you want.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.110
And those questions
come up all the time.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.360
Or I really want to
do solar power, how

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.650
much is it going to cost me?

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.390
And being able to give
someone a ballpark

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.970
by running through the numbers
really quickly is great.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.680
Or when you're in
a job interview,

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.230
and someone asks you these
sorts of questions, probably,

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.460
not necessarily, about
developing-country stuff,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.520
but just a random question.

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.210
Having that skill will
serve you in a job interview

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.070
when you're in a consulting
job, and a customer asks you

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.070
a question, when you're
in developing countries.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:17.190
It's going to be
useful, I swear.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.130
And it's pretty powerful
to be able to answer,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.000
instead of just being
like, I don't know.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.690
I wish I did.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:30.840
So I screwed up and mentioned
that the LED was in amp hours,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.880
and I sent you guys all an
email apologizing for that.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.310
So amp hours would be how
long a battery can last,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.270
and there was a
confusion about that.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:48.730
So for example, if a battery
has a 2-amp-hour rating,

00:05:48.730 --> 00:05:51.970
for example, which
is kind of low,

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.350
and you know voltage
of your battery,

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.990
or the voltage
that you're using,

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:02.170
and the current
that you're drawing.

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:05.960
Then you can use the
current to figure out,

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.990
so let's say it's 2
milliamps is your current,

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:17.110
then you can divide
the 2 amp hours.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:22.930
Divide it by 2
milliamps equals 1--

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:26.293
I really fail at
math on the board.

00:06:26.293 --> 00:06:27.460
Am I blocking in the camera?

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:27.960
Sorry.

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.750
So we have 1,000 hours
that is going to last,

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.432
and again, you have efficiency
issues with batteries

00:06:37.432 --> 00:06:39.140
that we'll talk about
a little bit today.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.440
So that's not an
exact number that you

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.390
can rely on, that
my battery is going

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:44.830
to last exactly 1,000 hours.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.670
But it gives you, again, an
order of magnitude estimate.

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.990
Yeah.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:56.470
So does anyone have a question
about amp hours versus amps?

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:59.140
Batteries?

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.720
Some of these Muddy
Card questions,

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.330
please keep putting questions
you have on the Muddy Cards.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:07.150
But if you're confused
about something,

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.450
also feel free to contact
me or meet and set up

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:14.317
a time to meet or come after
class or before class to talk.

00:07:14.317 --> 00:07:16.900
Because we're happy to help, and
we don't have explicit office

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:17.530
hours.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:20.930
But we are around a
lot and happy to help.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.530
So someone wrote like I
just want more insight

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:25.900
into power energy, time,
and work relationships,

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.810
and that's a hard question
to answer generically.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.945
But I'm happy to sit down with
you and give you more insight.

00:07:35.157 --> 00:07:37.490
And then some other quick
ones background of instructor,

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.860
I completely forgot to tell
you anything about myself

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.610
other than I have a
kid, which is probably

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.170
the least useful thing for you.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.240
So I apologize for that.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.720
So I'm a PhD in
mechanical engineering.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.290
I hope to God I'm
in my last year.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:52.590
I've been here a while.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.130
I did my undergrad at
MIT a long time ago,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:56.900
also in mechanical engineering.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.120
And when I finished,
I spent three years

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.650
in the automotive
industry working

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:02.998
for Texas Instruments,
which no longer has

00:08:02.998 --> 00:08:04.790
an automotive sensors
and controls division

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:06.350
but did at the time.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:08.900
They sold it off, and
now it's called Sensata.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.540
And so I worked at first
as a manufacturing engineer

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:14.840
and then as a design engineer,
building pressure switch

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.180
manifolds, which
go into vehicles

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.890
to ensure that the
car transmission is

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:24.950
in the right gear, so that
if you think you're in drive,

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:28.670
the car also agrees that you're
in drive and not in reverse,

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:31.740
for example, which is
pretty safety critical.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.840
And then switched over to
becoming a design engineer

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:39.830
and primarily worked on
capacitive automotive--

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:42.770
capacitive sensors
which are often

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:46.370
used for like anti-roll
issues to make sure the car

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:50.750
stays flat and parallel to the
ground and doesn't flip over.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:52.550
So I have design
and manufacturing

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.590
and industry experience
which was really good.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.290
I learned a ton, but
I decided that it

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.570
wasn't as fulfilling
or my passion

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.170
to be working in
automotive industry.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.680
I really wanted to be working
on things that were directly

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:05.880
if having humanitarian benefit.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.880
And while both of
those car sensors

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.970
help a lot to not get you
dead on the road in a car,

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.380
and that's pretty important,
it wasn't a good fit for me.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.360
So then I quit and
was a little lost,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.200
and through a variety of
things, found myself back at MIT

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:23.090
working for the Media
Lab in a group called

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:26.150
Think Cycle and
Design that Matters.

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:27.680
And Design that
Matters has spun off

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.860
into its own separate nonprofit
that's run in Kendall Square.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.300
Think Cycle is still a website.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.890
I'm not sure how
actively it's used,

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.240
but the idea was to do
collaborative design

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:41.000
work for developing
countries overseas.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.100
So that someone in
Kenya could post saying,

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:44.900
I need a better water
filtration system,

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:47.900
and someone working in the
US who had a few extra hours

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.290
could work on that problem.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.470
Which is interesting,
but, as we'll talk about,

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:53.270
it's really hard to
do that kind of work

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:57.030
without actually going to Kenya
or wherever you need to go.

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.970
So that was one
of the challenges.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:01.580
But through that,
I met Amy Smith

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.340
and met Sally [? Sesno-- ?] it's
in the Public Service Center--

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.250
and became MIT's first service
learning coordinator, which

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:10.970
allowed me to figure out
how to apply projects that

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.920
help people, both in
developing countries

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:18.170
as well as in this country,
people who are underserved

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:21.950
in a variety of
definitions, figure out

00:10:21.950 --> 00:10:23.897
how students at MIT
in their classes

00:10:23.897 --> 00:10:25.980
could be working on projects
that would help them.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.220
So for example,
how can you learn

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:34.400
in 2002, which is Mechanics
of Materials, relatively

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.410
introductory course,
how can you actually

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:37.670
be helping people in Haiti?

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:40.478
And figuring out
projects, activities

00:10:40.478 --> 00:10:42.770
that you're doing in class,
that you're learning really

00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:44.690
hardcore engineering
or hardcore science,

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:46.760
or whatever can actually
be helping people

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.230
in developing countries too.

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:50.762
Because that tends to resonate
really well with students,

00:10:50.762 --> 00:10:52.220
make the material
more interesting,

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.320
make you more excited
to learn the material,

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:55.410
and help people too.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.730
So it was cool, and I loved
doing it for three years.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.470
And then I said, you
know what, I really

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.210
want to be doing
these cool projects,

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.020
not just helping people do them.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:06.030
So that's why I came
back to grad school.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.623
And so my grad
school research is

00:11:07.623 --> 00:11:10.040
in charcoal, which you'll learn
a ton about in this class,

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:12.650
because it's very relevant
to the class, conveniently.

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:15.830
And then also studying
service learning

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:18.880
as a pedagogical
technique in engineering.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:19.880
So that's in a nutshell.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:24.460
Do you guys have any other
questions about my background?

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:25.080
Yeah?

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:27.205
AUDIENCE: Do you mind
sharing what year was it when

00:11:27.205 --> 00:11:28.270
the first--

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:31.442
what year was it when you were
the first service coordinator?

00:11:31.442 --> 00:11:32.650
Service learning coordinator?

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:33.442
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:11:33.442 --> 00:11:35.535
I think it was 2002
that I started that.

00:11:35.535 --> 00:11:35.920
Somewhere around there.

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:38.420
AUDIENCE: Everything is so far
developed down that road now.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:39.310
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:41.908 --> 00:11:42.700
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:43.030
Yeah.

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:43.570
No.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:45.280
MIT has changed a ton.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:50.170
Like I've been here
since '94, and MIT

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:56.170
has changed a ton in almost
two decades in a lot of ways.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:59.962
And many ways that I'm
quite jealous that I didn't

00:11:59.962 --> 00:12:01.420
have D-Lab when I
was an undergrad,

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:02.628
didn't have service learning.

00:12:02.628 --> 00:12:06.400
So it's great that we have it.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.860
And just to repeat
that question,

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:13.750
you were asking
when I came to MIT

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:15.940
or when I did the Public
Service Center work.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:16.810
So yeah.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:20.050
So any other
questions about that?

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:25.030
More details about
the trip we're

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:26.530
going to talk about today.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.870
Projects from last
year, and details

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.120
on the final project, a
little bit today and then more

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:32.380
next week.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:36.500
Extent of work the D-Lab
does in developing countries,

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:39.910
I think we work in
more than 20 countries.

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:42.910
The biggest work
we do is over IAP,

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:46.270
when a bunch of students
travel for D-Lab development,

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:48.190
as well as--

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:49.600
what other classes travel?

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:50.020
AUDIENCE: Cycle Ventures.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:51.228
AMY BANZAERT: Cycle Ventures.

00:12:51.228 --> 00:12:52.360
AUDIENCE: [? Education. ?]

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:53.152
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:12:53.152 --> 00:12:55.990
The D-Lab school's
class travels.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:58.570
So there's a lot of work
done over [? IP. ?] Then,

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.090
a bunch of students often get
fellowships to travel over

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.950
the summer to do work.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:06.700
Amy Smith and myself and
José Gómez-Márquez and some

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.460
of the other D-Lab staff
and instructors also travel

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.170
independently to do work
in developing countries.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:17.920
So we're working worldwide.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:20.050
We work on almost
every continent.

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.780
Not Australia and
not Antarctica,

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:25.810
but pretty much other than
that, we're working on it.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:31.288
And how we pick countries
is complicated and subtle.

00:13:31.288 --> 00:13:32.330
So I won't get into that.

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:34.080
But there's a lot of
countries we work in,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.770
and more are being
added regularly.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:39.022
However, we try to maintain
really strong relationships

00:13:39.022 --> 00:13:40.730
with the community
partners we work with.

00:13:40.730 --> 00:13:45.520
So we're not just doing one-off
visit one year and then leave

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:48.820
and never see the
community partner again,

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:52.030
because that's not very useful
for the community partner.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:54.910
That tends to do harm, not
good, for our relationship

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:58.300
and the general NGO
world's relationship

00:13:58.300 --> 00:13:59.290
to community partners.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:00.790
So we try to revisit.

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.450
So that requires a
lot of commitment.

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.305
So once we have an
established relationship,

00:14:04.305 --> 00:14:05.680
we have to keep
going back, which

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:09.550
means we can only grow if
we have additional capacity

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:13.030
to go back to new places.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:15.070
Are there any other
questions about that, just

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:16.330
general D-Lab stuff?

00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.430
I think there's
12 D-Lab classes,

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:21.820
so lots of opportunities
to faux major in D-Lab,

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:23.450
if you so choose.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.530
We have some students who
basically do that, or just

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:27.970
take one and experience it.

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:30.050
Enjoy it.

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.050
Yeah, there's Europe
opportunities, other stuff,

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.510
if you want to
get more involved.

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:36.160
OK.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:37.570
So that's Muddy Cards.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.580
So I will try to do
that relatively quickly.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.500
But at the beginning
of every day,

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:46.000
make sure we review what
wasn't clear last time.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:46.720
Yeah.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:51.380
So next, I want to
talk about the lantern.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:55.390
So as I mentioned,
they were awesome.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.355
Here's a picture of
almost all of them,

00:14:58.355 --> 00:14:59.980
and it was really
exciting to see them.

00:14:59.980 --> 00:15:02.422
And what I've done is I
put them on the back walls,

00:15:02.422 --> 00:15:03.880
and so what I'd
like everyone to do

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:07.000
is take two minutes to just--

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.970
if you haven't looked at
the sheet that I gave you

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:11.560
that gives you
feedback-- look at it.

00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:13.060
Look at the grading
rubrics, so you

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:14.560
have a sense of what we valued.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:18.760
And then check out the lanterns,
and see what strikes you,

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:21.010
both in terms of the
rubric as a really solid

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:23.940
or a really weak lantern.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:25.690
There's nothing really
weak on that table,

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:28.070
but there's some that
are stronger than others.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:31.870
And then also, ones
that just strike you

00:15:31.870 --> 00:15:35.260
as ones that are some of the
best in terms of actually using

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.350
them in developing
countries, what really fits.

00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:39.592
And try out the switches.

00:15:39.592 --> 00:15:41.050
Some are easier to
use than others,

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:43.090
or more obvious to
use than others.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:46.300
I think when we last played with
them, they were all working.

00:15:46.300 --> 00:15:48.250
Hopefully, they all still are.

00:15:48.250 --> 00:15:50.800
So just take a few minutes
just to familiarize yourself

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:55.697
with them, and I will give them
back to you after next week,

00:15:55.697 --> 00:15:56.780
but I need them this week.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:58.683
So I don't take them back yet.

00:15:58.683 --> 00:15:59.350
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.058
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:16:00.058 --> 00:16:03.098
So I'm just going to highlight
some of the cooler lanterns

00:16:03.098 --> 00:16:04.390
that some of the students made.

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.557
There's a lot of different
form factors you can see.

00:16:06.557 --> 00:16:08.440
This one would be
a personal lantern,

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:12.310
and then you switch it on,
and it has a nice reflector.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:19.278
There's also some that look very
much like traditional lanterns.

00:16:19.278 --> 00:16:21.320
One of the things I really
like about this design

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:24.710
is both that it has a very
simple handmade switch,

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:26.900
and you can see it turns
on and off like that.

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:30.410
And then also, the
top opens, so that you

00:16:30.410 --> 00:16:33.050
can look inside,
understand how it works,

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:35.910
and if there's a problem with
it, repair it very easily.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:39.230
Which is a nice aspect of
designing for any product,

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:42.260
and specifically in developing
countries, where getting help

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.330
for repair is harder.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:46.880
We also have some
more radical designs.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:50.270
For example, this I call
the light saber design,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:52.760
and the intent from
the student was

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:56.780
that it would help you
light your way at night.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:58.610
So you could hold it
as a walking stick,

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:02.690
and then also use it to light
whatever you needed to light.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:10.130
This design hasn't
been fully realized,

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:11.990
in terms of the
lantern aspect of it.

00:17:11.990 --> 00:17:16.099
But the use of the binder
clip as a switch is very cute,

00:17:16.099 --> 00:17:17.210
and we liked it a lot.

00:17:21.109 --> 00:17:23.700
This lantern uses a
ping pong ball diffuser,

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:28.369
which is a really nice
integration of found material

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:33.560
very effectively used to improve
the effectiveness of the light.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.170
And you can see the whole
range of devices here,

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:43.980
and we just really enjoyed
seeing the tremendous variation

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:46.590
in all the different
devices with students

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.090
all being given the
same assignment.

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:04.310
[END PLAYBACK]

00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:06.140
AMY BANZAERT: So the
variety is tremendous,

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.300
and it's really cool that
you got an assignment

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:11.360
to all make a lantern.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:15.535
And I really can't say there's
any two that look the same--

00:18:15.535 --> 00:18:16.160
not even close.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.130
And part of that's the reality
of found materials and this all

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:20.180
being different people.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:23.010
But it was nice
to see that range.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:27.350
And so clearly, there's not a
mental conception of lantern

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:29.360
that is so rigid
that we're all going

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:32.690
in the same direction, which
is pretty cool, actually,

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:37.400
because I talked to a woman
who works for the World

00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:39.920
Bank, focused on
lighting and [AUDIO OUT]

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:44.510
and they've really pushed lately
for better lanterns in Africa.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:47.870
Because 10 or 15 years
ago, all the lanterns

00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:51.410
basically looked like
Coleman camping lanterns.

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:53.990
Because that was the designer's
conception of lantern,

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:55.610
and that's what
they built. Which

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:58.790
is a really limiting
problematic situation

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:02.600
for the whole different range
of lighting needs in Africa.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:08.270
And Africa itself, we talked
about it, and it's a continent,

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.130
and it's a big continent.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:13.460
So it's like talking about the
needs of America's, including

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:15.230
North and South
America and saying,

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:18.560
oh, yeah, we all need
the same exact shoe.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:20.810
And no one in this room is
even wearing the same shoe,

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:23.630
because we all have different
needs just within this room,

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:27.110
and we're a pretty uniform
community in some respects.

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:29.660
In other respects
not so much, but in

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:31.190
some respects very uniform.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:32.690
And yet we all need
different shoes,

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:35.120
and similarly we might all
need different lanterns.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:38.913
And so if you abstract
that to-- or expand

00:19:38.913 --> 00:19:41.330
that to all of Africa and all
the different lantern needs,

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:41.870
that's huge.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:44.690
And yet all the lanterns are
looking like Coleman lanterns

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:45.800
or kerosene lanterns.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:46.672
It's very limiting.

00:19:46.672 --> 00:19:48.380
So I was really excited
to see the range.

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:51.840
What else did you guys notice?

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:53.618
AUDIENCE: The switches
were pretty unique.

00:19:53.618 --> 00:19:54.410
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:55.452
The switches were unique.

00:19:55.452 --> 00:20:00.950
Some were very easy to
understand, easy to use.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.640
Both handmade and off-the-shelf
ones that were grabbed.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:07.340
Others were pretty subtle
and hard to figure out,

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:08.300
is what I saw.

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.990
So there was a
real variety there.

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:11.642
And after class,
during the break,

00:20:11.642 --> 00:20:13.100
you might want to
go back and think

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:15.560
about what made
for a great switch

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.950
and what made for
a subtle switch.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:23.030
I know like, for
example, this lantern,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:27.260
the person wrote in their
write up intentionally

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:31.910
that they have the switch
right here to turn it on,

00:20:31.910 --> 00:20:33.223
and it's not working anymore.

00:20:33.223 --> 00:20:33.890
AUDIENCE: It is.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:34.490
AMY BANZAERT: Oh, it is?

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:35.000
Oh, OK.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.860
It is.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.240
And so they said, oh, but
that looks kind of ugly.

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:41.030
So I'm going to add
these wires here,

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:43.590
so that the aesthetic
is a little bit better.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.860
Which is a really nice
thought, except the result

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:47.690
is that the switch
is really buried.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:50.755
So you have no-- it took me
quite a while to figure out,

00:20:50.755 --> 00:20:52.130
without reading
the instructions,

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:55.070
and I didn't read the
instructions unless I had to.

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.940
Oh, how is this going to work?

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:00.140
It takes a while to
figure out, and there

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.080
is a realm of design where
it's appropriate to make

00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:03.950
things subtle.

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:05.645
But for the type of
design we're going

00:21:05.645 --> 00:21:08.270
to be doing, where we're working
with people who are definitely

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:10.228
not-- almost definitely
not going to be reading

00:21:10.228 --> 00:21:11.780
instructions, and
a variety of people

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.280
are going to be
using it, you always

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:17.750
want it to be very evident
how the device works,

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:23.240
so that people can use
it without instructions.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:24.575
Anything else you guys noticed?

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:27.750
Yeah.

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:29.100
AUDIENCE: I guess when I
approached the project,

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:32.010
I was like, OK, so she wants
a lantern not a flashlight.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:33.923
And so the challenging
part was in seeing

00:21:33.923 --> 00:21:36.215
I think we're all really
resourceful, because there are

00:21:36.215 --> 00:21:38.740
like [INAUDIBLE] and there
was, like, an eyeglass,

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:40.190
like a case for eyeglasses.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:42.810
So that's really fun to see.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:44.400
So it's challenging like to--

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:47.482
I really wanted to meet the
expectations of the assignment,

00:21:47.482 --> 00:21:49.440
but it was so broad that
I was like, oh my god,

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:50.490
what does she want us to do?

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:51.510
Should it be, like, be
channeled in one direction?

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:52.802
Should it [INAUDIBLE] radially?

00:21:52.802 --> 00:21:53.910
Oh, my gosh.

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:57.060
So I don't know how limited
we're going to be in our design

00:21:57.060 --> 00:21:58.600
in these developing countries.

00:21:58.600 --> 00:21:59.433
But--

00:21:59.433 --> 00:22:01.350
AMY BANZAERT: I think
this is very real world,

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:03.690
that no one says,
I want a flashlight

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:05.410
that looks exactly like this.

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:07.440
And even if they do,
they rarely mean,

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:10.290
and if it looks exactly like
this, I'll be happy with it.

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:14.280
Because when you're not a
designer or an engineer,

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:17.050
and you're talking
about a product,

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:19.323
it's really hard to talk
beyond what you've seen

00:22:19.323 --> 00:22:20.490
and what you've experienced.

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:22.410
And it's rare that what
you've seen and experienced

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:24.330
is sufficient for what
your actual needs are.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.700
So part of the
challenge is how do I

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.070
dig down, ask the
right questions,

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:31.920
talk to my users to really
understand what they need,

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.810
as opposed to what they say
they need, which may or may not

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:35.310
match?

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:36.727
And why you really
want to respect

00:22:36.727 --> 00:22:41.190
your user and their requests and
really understand the impetus

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:42.720
behind all their requests.

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:46.710
Some are really confusing to
you and really legitimate,

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:51.990
and others are really confusing
to you, or really clear to you,

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:54.150
and not entirely what they mean.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:58.200
So for example, we
were just talking

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.800
about this the other day in
D-Lab, that oftentimes women

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:03.450
who spend a lot of
hours collecting

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:07.260
water and/or collecting
wood value that time,

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.650
because it means they're
away from their family.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:10.650
They get a little break.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.570
It's the only time they
get away from the house,

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:14.362
and they get to hang
out with their friends

00:23:14.362 --> 00:23:15.630
and go for a long walk.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:17.380
And that can be a
really nice thing,

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:21.240
and so eliminating that
time without building

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:25.200
in the other opportunity
for that socialization

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:27.930
means they've just lost a
key part of their daily life

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:28.930
that they value.

00:23:28.930 --> 00:23:31.140
And so they may say,
oh, well, thanks

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:33.820
a lot for making this more
efficient stove or whatever,

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:36.900
but I would rather
keep using my old one.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.120
Not because I don't
like the new efficiency

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.520
or the new lack of
smoke, but because I

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:42.780
miss the time with my friends.

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:45.210
And that's so important to
my mental health and sanity

00:23:45.210 --> 00:23:48.030
and well-being that I'm still
going to go collect the wood.

00:23:48.030 --> 00:23:49.590
And so there's a
million examples

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:52.295
of why projects like this fail,
and so it's really important

00:23:52.295 --> 00:23:53.670
when you're talking
to your users

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:56.760
to understand all
these subtle elements.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:24:01.080
And so if someone might
very offhand mention,

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:02.760
it's nice to talk to my friends.

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:05.730
And if you don't pick up
on that as a huge need,

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:08.950
you may design something that's
going to flop for that reason.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:10.920
And so here, you didn't
really have a chance

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:12.000
to talk to any user.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.200
I just said, make a lantern.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.530
But that is the open-ended
thing that you often

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:18.450
get from customers.

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:19.540
So yeah.

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:20.040
OK.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:21.625
So overall, yeah, great job.

00:24:21.625 --> 00:24:22.875
You got the specific feedback.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:28.800
Yeah.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:29.300
OK.

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:31.745
So are there any other
questions or comments?

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:38.040
OK.

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.180
So I just wanted to do a little
review on the human power

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:41.870
stuff.

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:45.140
So you guys all got to
try out the tump line.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:49.610
You can see, where it actually
goes on your head varies.

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:51.920
These are both cloth tump lines.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:56.840
I've seen them made out of
bark, a variety of things.

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:00.860
Yeah, but you can see the
absurd amount of weight

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:03.020
that you can carry
with a tump line.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:06.440
Like these are
full Coke bottles,

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:11.840
and that is really a
ridiculous amount of weight.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.772
And the lab assignment
includes reading up

00:25:14.772 --> 00:25:16.980
on these a little bit more,
so I'm to let you do that

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:19.510
and not talk about them right
now, but they're very cool.

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:20.510
Did you have a question?

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:23.290
AUDIENCE: How long do they
walk with that [INAUDIBLE]??

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:26.330
AMY BANZAERT: All day.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:28.130
Yeah.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:36.308
And then the head carry, so you
can see the variety of devices.

00:25:36.308 --> 00:25:37.850
There's not the huge
variety, But you

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:39.980
do see like both the
[? Jerry ?] can and the more

00:25:39.980 --> 00:25:41.360
traditional water carrying.

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.180
And you see all sorts
of ridiculous things

00:25:44.180 --> 00:25:45.950
or amazing things
on people's head.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:50.780
And the thickness
of this fabric part

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:54.380
that separates your head from
whatever you're carrying really

00:25:54.380 --> 00:25:58.963
varies, depending
on cultural norms.

00:25:58.963 --> 00:26:01.130
So you'll see a whole range
from like you can barely

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:03.560
see it to something like
this, which is quite thick.

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:05.760
We use quite thick just
because I find it easier,

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:07.970
and I figure you'll
find it easier too.

00:26:07.970 --> 00:26:10.800
It's pretty normal to get
help bringing it up and down.

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:13.400
So getting that
help wasn't a cheat,

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.400
but it's also pretty normal
to be using no hands,

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:21.140
and none of us were able to
pull that off, unsurprisingly.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:23.060
One thing we didn't
really talk about

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:27.010
is pedal power, which is a
huge part of human power.

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:28.760
And that's a little
bit of your assignment

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:30.718
too, so I'm not going to
go into that too much.

00:26:34.580 --> 00:26:36.290
This whole thing is
from Jody [? Wu, ?]

00:26:36.290 --> 00:26:39.230
who was one of those D-Lab
majors I talked about,

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:42.440
who took as many D-Lab classes
as she could take, and is now

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:44.640
working on a company.

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:49.880
She owns a company in Tanzania,
doing pedal-power devices that

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:52.928
came out of her D-Lab work.

00:26:52.928 --> 00:26:54.470
And there's a whole
variety of people

00:26:54.470 --> 00:26:56.000
where these pictures came from.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.970
But so pedal power, all
the way from the bicycle,

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:01.850
which you're used
to, a completely home

00:27:01.850 --> 00:27:05.180
built bicycle, which would
be rather challenging to ride

00:27:05.180 --> 00:27:07.460
but is pretty amazing.

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:12.200
To something like a bicycle but
is just basically using wheels.

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:17.990
Both for going down hills and
up hills, because carrying loads

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:22.190
is always an issue, and the
assumption of good roads

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:24.950
is not a good one.

00:27:24.950 --> 00:27:29.000
To a pedal-powered device that
looks kind of like a sewing

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:32.270
machine power, but
instead is powering--

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:34.198
the-- what is it? $100 laptop?

00:27:34.198 --> 00:27:35.240
Is that what it's called?

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:35.970
Or $200 now?

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:36.470
I forget.

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:37.860
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:37.860 --> 00:27:38.990
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, OLPC.

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:39.560
That's it.

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:41.180
Thanks.

00:27:41.180 --> 00:27:42.590
Yeah.

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:48.060
This is a pedal-powered
compressor,

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.410
which is an interesting
project, because pedal power is

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:55.610
the exactly wrong
technology to use here.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:59.660
Because when the
pressure tank is empty,

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:02.420
you have to be
pedaling incredibly

00:28:02.420 --> 00:28:04.220
quickly to get any
pressure built up,

00:28:04.220 --> 00:28:07.400
because there's no back pressure
for you to pedal against.

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:13.190
And when it's full, you have
an infinite amount of pressure

00:28:13.190 --> 00:28:16.730
to push against, and so
you can't pedal at all.

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.790
And in fact, there's
a really handy device

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:22.370
that works for this great
that uses arm power,

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:24.500
and of course, you could
think of a foot version.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:28.370
But this is just a reminder that
pedal power has a near infinite

00:28:28.370 --> 00:28:30.410
number of uses, but
not literally infinite.

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:35.460
There are some places where
pedal power is ridiculous.

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:42.830
This is a pedal-powered
grinder, I think for corn.

00:28:42.830 --> 00:28:46.170
People carry amazing
loads on bikes as well,

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:47.810
and these are bikes
specially modified

00:28:47.810 --> 00:28:50.660
to allow for carrying loads.

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:54.230
We usually think of pedal
power as two-wheeled bikes,

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:56.870
but three wheels is
really common too

00:28:56.870 --> 00:29:00.050
and can offer a lot
of stability benefits.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:04.340
Pedal-powered
ambulances, there's

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:07.910
a lot of places in this
country, and more so in Europe,

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:11.330
where pedal power is standard
in more developed countries

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:12.450
as well.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:14.450
And we're seeing it more
in Cambridge, actually.

00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:15.992
There's a lot of
pedal-power delivery

00:29:15.992 --> 00:29:18.260
now in Cambridge, which is
kind of cool, though harder

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:21.530
right now in this season,
with all our snow.

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:24.170
Variety of folding bikes
or different orientations

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:29.330
of bikes, a variety of
different things carrying,

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:33.740
amazing bikes for
going on water.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:39.140
These are pedal-powered
washing machines that were--

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:40.760
I don't know about
this one, but there

00:29:40.760 --> 00:29:42.470
was a collaboration
between D-Lab

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.170
and a group in Guatemala
that does pedal power

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:47.960
to build a washing machine.

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:50.930
Pedal-powered water
pumps, coffee de-pulper,

00:29:50.930 --> 00:29:56.510
maize sheller, washing machine,
blender, plow, knife sharpener,

00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:59.510
soap blender, nut sheller, saw.

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:00.980
More washing machine pics.

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:02.570
This is the group in Guatemala.

00:30:05.450 --> 00:30:10.190
Maize grinder, again,
woodworking lathe, corn

00:30:10.190 --> 00:30:12.830
shelling, so there's
a ridiculous number

00:30:12.830 --> 00:30:14.420
of ways to use pedal power.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:18.070
And because most of you
hopefully have ridden a bike,

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:19.820
and there's a lot of
technologies in D-Lab

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:22.765
that are more familiar with
pedal power than the pumping

00:30:22.765 --> 00:30:23.390
that we showed.

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:25.720
That's why we went for pumping
rather than pedal power.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:28.690
But it is something
to keep in mind,

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:30.280
as you're thinking
about projects,

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:34.300
that pedal power is pretty
powerful and awesome.

00:30:34.300 --> 00:30:37.810
Any questions on that?

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:38.530
OK.

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:43.280
So now, we're going to
talk about power generally,

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.460
and one of the
biggest challenges

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:49.930
is that power is really
generated where you need it

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.470
or when you need it.

00:30:51.470 --> 00:30:56.260
So for example, the sun is
only out for some portion

00:30:56.260 --> 00:31:00.040
of the day, and in the winter,
in New England, even less.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:02.920
And in Norway, where my
brother lives, even less,

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:06.200
and on super cloudy days,
it's a whole lot less useful.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:11.110
So that's a question of when,
and that's true for wind

00:31:11.110 --> 00:31:13.120
as well, the when is an issue.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:14.680
And then the where is an issue.

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:16.870
Very few people want
to live right next door

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:19.540
to a nuclear power plant
or a coal-fired plant,

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:21.160
nor is it preferable to.

00:31:21.160 --> 00:31:23.920
And for like
microhydro systems, you

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:26.530
may not want to live
right on the river.

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:28.550
You may want to
live a bit further.

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:32.830
So what are the solutions
that humanity has come up with

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:36.370
to address that issue?

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:37.948
AUDIENCE: Power grid.

00:31:37.948 --> 00:31:38.740
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:39.580
So we have grids.

00:31:42.748 --> 00:31:44.587
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
transmission works.

00:31:44.587 --> 00:31:46.795
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, which
include transmission lines.

00:31:52.916 --> 00:31:54.791
AUDIENCE: Ways to store
the power before they

00:31:54.791 --> 00:31:56.340
had to transport it.

00:31:56.340 --> 00:31:57.780
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:31:57.780 --> 00:32:01.460
And then we have
storage and transport.

00:32:03.990 --> 00:32:05.100
Absolutely.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:10.240
And so with-- so we're going to
talk about both of those today.

00:32:10.240 --> 00:32:13.770
And so what are the
limitations of grids?

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.050
What are the challenges
you face with any grid?

00:32:19.578 --> 00:32:21.370
AUDIENCE: It's difficult
to actually set up

00:32:21.370 --> 00:32:23.330
a the large-scale grid.

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:27.970
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, but what
do you mean by difficult?

00:32:27.970 --> 00:32:31.540
AUDIENCE: Well it's
very money intensive,

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:34.240
if you want to string up
a highly efficient grid.

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:37.540
It's the main reason why the
US is actually still working

00:32:37.540 --> 00:32:41.548
on a grid system developed,
like, a hundred years ago.

00:32:41.548 --> 00:32:42.340
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:32:42.340 --> 00:32:44.890
So grids are incredibly
capital intensive,

00:32:44.890 --> 00:32:46.790
if you're doing a big grid.

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:47.290
Juan.

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:49.330
AUDIENCE: Change of
voltage, as you go across

00:32:49.330 --> 00:32:51.340
you have to increase
it for long distance

00:32:51.340 --> 00:32:53.350
and then decrease it
back to usable power.

00:32:53.350 --> 00:32:56.830
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, and do why
you have the change of voltage?

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:58.960
AUDIENCE: Efficiency.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:00.718
When you're transferring
it through the--

00:33:00.718 --> 00:33:01.510
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:02.010
Yeah.

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:07.780
So you have loss issues that
you need to take into account.

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:08.810
I saw a lot of hands.

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:09.310
Yeah.

00:33:09.310 --> 00:33:13.680
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] So part
of the-- one of the smart grid

00:33:13.680 --> 00:33:18.310
technologies is [INAUDIBLE] to
the phase meter [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:18.310 --> 00:33:20.563
But in reality, like, today,
all they have is, like,

00:33:20.563 --> 00:33:22.480
people at different
substations, and just like

00:33:22.480 --> 00:33:24.147
crank it up, crank
it down a little bit,

00:33:24.147 --> 00:33:25.930
depending on how it's
coming [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:26.380
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:33:26.380 --> 00:33:28.422
AUDIENCE: Although I think
the problem with grids

00:33:28.422 --> 00:33:30.700
from a policy
standpoint is that,

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:32.380
once the infrastructure
is there,

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:34.390
they are there for
long periods of years.

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:35.890
And so it's really
hard to regulate,

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:40.050
because you have so many
different [INAUDIBLE] regions.

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:42.680
So these are transmission lines
going across different states.

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:45.180
Then, you get to the issue of
is it the federal government

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:45.840
or is it us?

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:48.410
And so just, like,
imagine regulating

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:51.628
all those policies [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:51.628 --> 00:33:52.670
AMY BANZAERT: Absolutely.

00:33:52.670 --> 00:33:55.780
So we have issues with,
once they're in place,

00:33:55.780 --> 00:33:58.480
they're in place forever.

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:00.040
The efficiency,
you can do things

00:34:00.040 --> 00:34:02.260
to improve the
efficiency, but you're

00:34:02.260 --> 00:34:04.030
going to have losses always.

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:09.440
And then policy and ownership
issues are challenging as well.

00:34:09.440 --> 00:34:11.219
I saw a few other hands.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:11.719
Yeah.

00:34:11.719 --> 00:34:14.177
AUDIENCE: When there's storms,
and you have a power outage,

00:34:14.177 --> 00:34:14.860
it's a problem.

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:15.652
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:34:15.652 --> 00:34:25.179
So storms, and I'd say more
generally maintenance for--

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:29.320
typical and atypical
situations is always an issue.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:29.980
Any others?

00:34:33.469 --> 00:34:34.689
Yeah, Missy.

00:34:34.689 --> 00:34:37.324
AUDIENCE: You usually have to
use it [INAUDIBLE] generate,

00:34:37.324 --> 00:34:40.788
like, [INAUDIBLE] or like--

00:34:40.788 --> 00:34:41.580
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:48.440
So it's-- there is no
storage in a typical grid.

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:52.790
So lack of storage is an issue.

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:55.820
Yeah, and then getting
to storage and transport,

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:59.480
and for that, we're going
to focus on batteries.

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:03.020
There are a few other
options, but the most common

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:04.160
is batteries.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:08.226
So what are some issues
around batteries?

00:35:08.226 --> 00:35:09.838
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] good life.

00:35:09.838 --> 00:35:10.630
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:15.076
So the life is an issue.

00:35:15.076 --> 00:35:16.300
AUDIENCE: Disposal.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:19.630
AMY BANZAERT: Disposal,
which is very linked to life.

00:35:27.305 --> 00:35:28.930
AUDIENCE: According
to our problem set,

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:31.140
it actually doesn't
carry that much energy.

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:33.468
So it's not very good.

00:35:33.468 --> 00:35:34.260
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:35:34.260 --> 00:35:39.900
So the density, the energy
density of batteries,

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:43.290
it's pretty poor compared to
a lot of other technologies

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:44.820
we're used to.

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:46.590
Any other thoughts?

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:47.123
Yeah.

00:35:47.123 --> 00:35:49.040
AUDIENCE: The materials
are like that you need

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:50.600
to use and make the battery.

00:35:50.600 --> 00:35:51.392
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:35:51.392 --> 00:35:55.880
So the materials,
that can be an issue

00:35:55.880 --> 00:35:59.660
for environmental reasons.

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:08.980
It can be an issue for
cost and just availability.

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:11.650
There are some materials
we use that we literally

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:15.280
have a fixed amount of,
and we're running out.

00:36:19.320 --> 00:36:20.440
Some other things?

00:36:20.440 --> 00:36:20.940
Yeah.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:21.452
Go ahead.

00:36:21.452 --> 00:36:23.910
AUDIENCE: One more thing the
batteries is just their design

00:36:23.910 --> 00:36:25.890
constraints, as far as
usually you use them

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:27.270
for small portable devices.

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:31.000
Yet the smaller the battery,
the less energy it provides.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:35.745
So it's just the [? naivete ?]
that design problems.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:44.575
AMY BANZAERT: Well, I don't
if it's exactly the right way

00:36:44.575 --> 00:36:46.325
to say it, but the way
I'm going to say it

00:36:46.325 --> 00:36:48.290
is that the size and the
weight of the battery

00:36:48.290 --> 00:36:51.110
versus its utility
to you is inversely

00:36:51.110 --> 00:36:53.550
proportional and
a bit of a bummer.

00:36:53.550 --> 00:36:58.730
So you want, for getting a
really good storage capability,

00:36:58.730 --> 00:37:01.790
you generally need a big,
large, heavy battery.

00:37:01.790 --> 00:37:04.670
And typically, you want as
light and small of battery

00:37:04.670 --> 00:37:06.420
as possible.

00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:10.040
So that's a pretty
frustrating conflict.

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:12.620
Yeah.

00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:14.210
Let's start with grids.

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:17.400
So all of these
are major issues,

00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:24.850
and I want to start by
talking about the US grid

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:25.660
just for a minute.

00:37:34.730 --> 00:37:37.090
So this is what the
US grid looks like.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:42.580
This is a really nice
interactive graph from NPR.

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:47.020
The link is on the slide
notes, and you can go to it

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:49.720
and play with it.

00:37:49.720 --> 00:37:53.090
And you can see all of the
different transmission lines,

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:56.860
and all these voltages are
incredibly high compared

00:37:56.860 --> 00:37:58.300
to what you're used to using.

00:37:58.300 --> 00:38:01.240
But you can see there's
a range of sizes,

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:07.810
because the higher you have
to transform the voltage,

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:10.540
the more expensive it is.

00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:13.450
But you can see,
this is our major--

00:38:13.450 --> 00:38:18.230
these are our major currents
on the eastern seaboard.

00:38:18.230 --> 00:38:21.430
So that is just an
interesting thing to note.

00:38:21.430 --> 00:38:25.990
And then in terms of the
power plants in the US,

00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:29.350
you can see just how
spread out they are

00:38:29.350 --> 00:38:30.790
and how varying in size.

00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:33.340
And therefore, how
critical our grid system

00:38:33.340 --> 00:38:38.020
is in order to get the
power to where we need it.

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:40.070
Otherwise, people in
Maine, for example,

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:42.750
would not have
much power at all,

00:38:42.750 --> 00:38:44.500
and you can see other
parts of the country

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:48.655
where the power would be
really limited otherwise.

00:38:48.655 --> 00:38:50.530
And then this is just
I just want to show you

00:38:50.530 --> 00:38:53.020
this, because it goes
down and shows you

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:55.150
all the different plant
types you can have.

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:58.480
And then you can
click on the plants,

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:03.620
you can go to each state and
compare where we get our power.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:06.250
So for example,
in Massachusetts,

00:39:06.250 --> 00:39:10.630
for primarily gas, whereas
in Vermont, they're

00:39:10.630 --> 00:39:11.710
primarily nuclear.

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:14.860
So it's just really interesting
to see, even within the US,

00:39:14.860 --> 00:39:17.830
how much variation
there is in states

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:21.920
right next to each other,
let alone across the country.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:27.310
You can also see what
