00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:05.780
than what we've got right now.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:08.170
So yeah.

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:11.260
This is just a very
cool demo to give you

00:40:11.260 --> 00:40:12.970
a sense of the US
grid really quickly.

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:20.380
OK.

00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:27.730
So now, it's time to do
some equations to think

00:40:27.730 --> 00:40:30.950
about the line losses that
you have to deal with.

00:40:30.950 --> 00:40:35.870
So the line losses
are all due to what?

00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:36.623
AUDIENCE: Heating.

00:40:36.623 --> 00:40:38.290
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah,
heating of the wire.

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:39.730
And why is the
wire being heated?

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:40.660
AUDIENCE: Current throught it.

00:40:40.660 --> 00:40:42.660
AMY BANZAERT: Because it
has current through it,

00:40:42.660 --> 00:40:44.380
and a wire has some resistance.

00:40:44.380 --> 00:40:49.300
So the equation for
that is Joule's Law,

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:53.230
and is defined as Q is equal
to-- which is the heat--

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:56.710
is to the square
of current times

00:40:56.710 --> 00:41:01.420
the resistance times the
time, and that's Joule's Law.

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:08.770
And if you're not
super-comfortable

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:10.630
with your double-E,
you're probably

00:41:10.630 --> 00:41:13.390
going to say, oh, well, the I
R, that sounds like a voltage,

00:41:13.390 --> 00:41:18.195
and throw a voltage in there,
and you're missing a subtlety.

00:41:18.195 --> 00:41:19.570
Because then you'd
say, oh, well,

00:41:19.570 --> 00:41:21.940
it's related to current and
voltage at the same level.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:24.460
But you have to figure out what
voltage you're calculating,

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:26.610
because if you
recall, while current

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:29.110
is going through the whole wire
at the same rate everywhere,

00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:31.600
because it's a flow,
a voltage is based

00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:33.700
on a comparison of two places.

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:36.070
And you have to compare
the right voltage in order

00:41:36.070 --> 00:41:38.433
to plug that into the
equation correctly.

00:41:38.433 --> 00:41:40.100
So we're not going
to do that right now,

00:41:40.100 --> 00:41:45.190
but we're going to go through
this equation in a little

00:41:45.190 --> 00:41:45.890
more detail.

00:41:45.890 --> 00:41:48.110
So hopefully, it becomes
a little more intuitive.

00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:50.440
So let's pretend
that we're trying

00:41:50.440 --> 00:41:54.130
to deliver power from some
power plant in western Mass

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:56.740
to Boston.

00:41:56.740 --> 00:42:00.610
And so we're going to call
that power that we need power

00:42:00.610 --> 00:42:07.480
with a capital P. And power,
as we recall, is the voltage,

00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:14.590
and that's from the
line to ground times

00:42:14.590 --> 00:42:21.160
the resistance through the
power line, so P equals VI.

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:24.858
But we also have the
issue of the power loss

00:42:24.858 --> 00:42:26.650
that we know is happening
through the wire.

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:29.500
So to calculate that you're
using the same equation.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:32.980
We're going to call
that lowercase p is

00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:38.822
equal to voltage from
the end of the line

00:42:38.822 --> 00:42:40.030
to the beginning of the line.

00:42:40.030 --> 00:42:43.420
So L1 to L2, times I.

00:42:43.420 --> 00:42:48.040
These I's, thankfully,
for just thinking--

00:42:48.040 --> 00:42:50.800
for minimizing how much
confusion, are the same I.

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:53.445
They're the same current
going through the wire, which

00:42:53.445 --> 00:42:55.570
is going to be the same
throughout the entire wire.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:58.180
But the voltage here,
for big P, we're

00:42:58.180 --> 00:43:00.040
worrying about
checking the voltage

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:02.410
at one end of the line
or the other to ground.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:05.770
And little p, we're worrying
about the voltage drop from one

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:08.330
end of the line to the other.

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:13.060
So for example, let's say
the power plant is providing

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:16.138
10,000 volts [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:16.138 --> 00:43:19.540
Like, where on the boat can I
write, and you can still see?

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:22.780
Like, if I write down to
there, can you see it?

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:24.910
No?

00:43:24.910 --> 00:43:27.130
No is a better answer
than blank stare.

00:43:27.130 --> 00:43:28.450
So all right.

00:43:28.450 --> 00:43:30.387
Can you see this range?

00:43:30.387 --> 00:43:30.970
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:43:30.970 --> 00:43:31.678
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:43:31.678 --> 00:43:33.502
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:33.502 --> 00:43:34.210
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:43:39.270 --> 00:43:40.875
And this is going
to make me batty.

00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:49.420
OK.

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:54.680
So we have 10,000 volts.

00:43:54.680 --> 00:44:01.730
So the voltage here from line
to ground at the power plant

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:12.180
is 10,000, and then let's say
we lose 1,000 volts across here

00:44:12.180 --> 00:44:13.210
due to line losses.

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:22.630
So that this would be this V,
and this would be V L minus G.

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:25.130
And then conveniently,
so then we

00:44:25.130 --> 00:44:27.640
know we have the V at the end.

00:44:27.640 --> 00:44:37.110
It's just going
to be 9,000 volts.

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:42.330
So this is pretty
simple math, hopefully.

00:44:42.330 --> 00:44:50.600
So power, the lowercase p,
is equal to the voltage drop

00:44:50.600 --> 00:45:05.690
across the line, so V
L1 to L2 times I. Yeah.

00:45:05.690 --> 00:45:06.190
Sorry.

00:45:06.190 --> 00:45:07.810
I'm getting myself confused.

00:45:07.810 --> 00:45:09.520
So power is equal to--

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:15.180
power is also equal
to I squared times R--

00:45:15.180 --> 00:45:25.010
that I. So our options
are we can make our V--

00:45:25.010 --> 00:45:25.510
sorry.

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:28.643
I just got totally
lost in my notes.

00:45:28.643 --> 00:45:29.560
So let me start again.

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:38.370
Right.

00:45:38.370 --> 00:45:39.610
OK.

00:45:39.610 --> 00:45:43.080
So we have these two equations.

00:45:43.080 --> 00:45:46.590
We're not going to worry
about numbers right now.

00:45:46.590 --> 00:45:50.945
So we want to maximize
this P and minimize that p.

00:45:54.950 --> 00:46:00.170
And we know the relationship
between these two V's.

00:46:00.170 --> 00:46:05.390
So then, so if we
minimize I, we minimize

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:08.220
that power, which is good.

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:10.790
But we also minimize
this power, which is bad.

00:46:10.790 --> 00:46:13.760
And that's why we're trying
to pop up the voltage

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:19.190
to be as high as possible,
so that we can maximize

00:46:19.190 --> 00:46:21.230
power and minimize our losses.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:22.910
Does that make
sense to everyone?

00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:25.410
I know, I got lost
in the middle.

00:46:25.410 --> 00:46:25.910
OK.

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:27.650
If it doesn't,
and you don't want

00:46:27.650 --> 00:46:30.590
to talk about it right now,
you can talk to me after class.

00:46:30.590 --> 00:46:32.510
We can go through it then.

00:46:32.510 --> 00:46:36.560
So that is great in a macrogrid.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:40.250
So we can bump up the voltage,
super, and for all set.

00:46:40.250 --> 00:46:44.990
However, in a microgrid,
bumping up the voltage,

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:48.540
transforming the voltage is
really, really expensive.

00:46:48.540 --> 00:46:50.900
So in a microgrid,
you don't do that.

00:46:50.900 --> 00:46:56.000
You just use your given voltage,
which saves you a lot of money

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.160
but also means that
line losses are huge.

00:46:58.160 --> 00:47:04.340
And so for in that case, if
you can't change your voltage,

00:47:04.340 --> 00:47:06.770
what is your loss based on?

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:12.490
You already answered it.

00:47:12.490 --> 00:47:15.200
It's the resistance in the wire
is what we have to worry about.

00:47:15.200 --> 00:47:22.240
So the resistance in the wire
is based on rho L over A,

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:28.000
where rho is equal to
the inherent resistivity,

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:33.820
L is the length,
and A is the area

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:36.470
in cross section of your wire.

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:39.068
And so for your
resistivity, that

00:47:39.068 --> 00:47:40.360
means you're picking your wire.

00:47:40.360 --> 00:47:42.820
So you could use an aluminum
wire or a copper wire

00:47:42.820 --> 00:47:45.940
or what have you.

00:47:45.940 --> 00:47:48.910
Copper tends to have the best
resistivity of the options,

00:47:48.910 --> 00:47:52.660
but copper is also
incredibly expensive,

00:47:52.660 --> 00:47:54.520
if for no other reason
then because people

00:47:54.520 --> 00:47:57.282
are stealing copper all
over countries and the US.

00:47:57.282 --> 00:47:59.740
So people who have beautiful
rain gutters made of copper no

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:01.573
longer do, because
people are stealing them,

00:48:01.573 --> 00:48:05.470
because copper is scarce
and expensive these days.

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:09.305
Length, so copper,
your resistivity

00:48:09.305 --> 00:48:10.930
is probably going to
be basically fixed

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:13.390
based on your costs.

00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:17.140
Length is something you can
control but is also a bummer.

00:48:17.140 --> 00:48:19.480
Because if you go too far--

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:22.000
if you want to
power a house that's

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:24.760
an extra few blocks
down, you can't if you're

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:25.750
limited by your length.

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:28.370
And then your area,
your diameter, again,

00:48:28.370 --> 00:48:31.710
is going to be completely
related to cost.

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:35.150
So from a cost standpoint,
you want a really small area,

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:38.970
but to minimize your resistance,
you want a really large area.

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:43.130
So yeah.

00:48:43.130 --> 00:48:46.700
So let's run through
the calculation just

00:48:46.700 --> 00:48:48.590
to hopefully make this clearer.

00:48:52.260 --> 00:49:00.490
So in teams of a
couple of people,

00:49:00.490 --> 00:49:02.390
if you can run through this.

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:06.850
So typical jumper
cables are helping

00:49:06.850 --> 00:49:10.420
to provide a jump to
a dead car battery

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:12.640
from an existing
working car battery.

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:16.990
So you're providing somewhere
on the order of 400 amps

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:23.410
at 12 volts, and the typical
diameter of a jumper cable

00:49:23.410 --> 00:49:30.340
is a wire gauge of 4, which
translates to 2/10 of an inch

00:49:30.340 --> 00:49:31.360
in diameter.

00:49:31.360 --> 00:49:35.770
Versus a typical wire like
you used on your lanterns

00:49:35.770 --> 00:49:39.070
is on the order of
a wire gauge of 26,

00:49:39.070 --> 00:49:43.580
which is a much, much,
much smaller diameter.

00:49:43.580 --> 00:49:46.480
And so we're going to
assume are using copper.

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:48.640
So that's the copper
resistivity length,

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:52.600
and so now what I want
you to do is figure out

00:49:52.600 --> 00:49:57.340
what length of cable can be
used for each wire gauge,

00:49:57.340 --> 00:49:59.170
so that you still--

00:49:59.170 --> 00:50:02.080
so that your losses
don't dominate over

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:04.020
your transmission.

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:05.440
Does this question make sense?

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:09.000
I'm seeing a lot
of confused faces.

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:09.793
Yeah?

00:50:09.793 --> 00:50:12.260
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:50:12.260 --> 00:50:13.400
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:16.070
So let's say, we can't lose--

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:18.440
you can define how
much you can lose,

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:21.740
because again, this is
open ended, intentionally

00:50:21.740 --> 00:50:22.410
open ended.

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:25.950
But if you're losing 100%--

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:29.670
if all your power is going
to heat, that's a problem.

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:33.810
And why don't you set what
percent can go to heat

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:35.865
and then figure out
what length you can use.

00:50:46.690 --> 00:50:47.710
OK.

00:50:47.710 --> 00:50:50.090
So I think most people are done.

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:54.080
So I'm just going to get a range
of where we are in the class.

00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:56.950
So can someone
just shout out what

00:50:56.950 --> 00:51:01.270
you got for the 26 gauge, the 4
gauge, and the percentage loss

00:51:01.270 --> 00:51:03.510
you assumed?

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:04.450
Like I know you guys--

