but getting the gist
down, and it looks good.

00:52:14.100 --> 00:52:16.342
Is there a team that
got wildly different?

00:52:16.342 --> 00:52:17.550
I think there's at least one.

00:52:17.550 --> 00:52:20.580
AUDIENCE: We got 0.36

00:52:20.580 --> 00:52:23.142
AMY BANZAERT: 0.36
meters and what?

00:52:23.142 --> 00:52:24.600
AUDIENCE: We didn't
calculate the--

00:52:24.600 --> 00:52:26.642
AMY BANZAERT: OK, and what
was your percent loss?

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:30.150
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.980
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:32.980 --> 00:52:34.140
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:34.140 --> 00:52:35.140
AMY BANZAERT: I'm sorry?

00:52:35.140 --> 00:52:36.440
AUDIENCE: 33%

00:52:36.440 --> 00:52:37.160
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:38.900
AUDIENCE: 33%

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:39.762
AMY BANZAERT: 33%.

00:52:39.762 --> 00:52:42.600
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:42.600 --> 00:52:45.254
AUDIENCE: Sorry, no, it's 1/6.

00:52:45.254 --> 00:52:47.650
We lost 2 votes out of 12 votes.

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:48.298
That's 1/6.

00:52:48.298 --> 00:52:49.340
AUDIENCE: No, power loss.

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:50.420
[INAUDIBLE]

00:52:50.420 --> 00:52:52.923
AUDIENCE: Is it according to
the power loss or voltage?

00:52:52.923 --> 00:52:53.756
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:53.756 --> 00:52:54.218
AUDIENCE: Same thing.

00:52:54.218 --> 00:52:55.218
AUDIENCE: It's the same.

00:52:55.218 --> 00:52:56.510
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:56.510 --> 00:52:57.210
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:52:57.210 --> 00:52:58.165
AUDIENCE: So it's 1/6.

00:52:58.165 --> 00:52:58.707
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:52:58.707 --> 00:52:59.350
Never mind.

00:53:03.050 --> 00:53:03.953
AMY BANZAERT: OK.

00:53:03.953 --> 00:53:08.300
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:08.300 --> 00:53:14.735
So basically, this is on
the order of a length of--

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:21.200
a jumper cable is usually
between 2 and 3 meters.

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:23.390
And these are all
really big losses.

00:53:23.390 --> 00:53:26.090
Like, your wires are going
to get super dangerously hot,

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:28.280
if you're losing
this much power.

00:53:28.280 --> 00:53:30.590
Which is fine for just doing
an order of magnitude--

00:53:30.590 --> 00:53:31.370
where am I?

00:53:31.370 --> 00:53:34.910
But that's why they're actually
quite a bit shorter than that.

00:53:34.910 --> 00:53:40.460
Plus, you rarely need your
cars to be 20 meters apart,

00:53:40.460 --> 00:53:42.371
thankfully--

00:53:42.371 --> 00:53:46.200
or when you do, it's
really frustrating.

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:50.150
And then this is a reason why
jumper cables are expensive

00:53:50.150 --> 00:53:54.290
and you need a big
diameter copper wire.

00:53:54.290 --> 00:53:55.740
This just wouldn't work.

00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:57.847
And so if someone, again,
consultant came to you,

00:53:57.847 --> 00:53:59.930
or someone came to you
looking for money, saying I

00:53:59.930 --> 00:54:01.638
have a great idea for
a new jumper cable,

00:54:01.638 --> 00:54:03.347
and we're going to
use really thin wires.

00:54:03.347 --> 00:54:04.880
It's going to save
us so much money.

00:54:04.880 --> 00:54:06.880
You can do this type of
calculation really fast.

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:10.160
It'd be like, oh, no,
you can't, unless you

00:54:10.160 --> 00:54:13.260
want to have magic cars that
are right next to each other.

00:54:13.260 --> 00:54:14.640
So yeah.

00:54:14.640 --> 00:54:16.760
AUDIENCE: If you want to
send it [INAUDIBLE] away,

00:54:16.760 --> 00:54:19.563
and so you just put, like,
two jumper cables lined up,

00:54:19.563 --> 00:54:21.230
there isn't supposed
to be a loss there.

00:54:21.230 --> 00:54:24.470
But do you know what the actual
loss is, when you, like, just

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:29.870
connect wire to wire for this
kind of power going through it?

00:54:29.870 --> 00:54:32.570
AMY BANZAERT: Well,
so if you connected--

00:54:32.570 --> 00:54:35.520
if you have your one
car and your next--

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:38.628
excuse me.

00:54:38.628 --> 00:54:40.670
If you have one car and
your next car, and you're

00:54:40.670 --> 00:54:42.110
connecting your jumper cables.

00:54:42.110 --> 00:54:45.860
And then you say so
that's just going to work,

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:47.390
because it's a
normal jumper cables.

00:54:47.390 --> 00:54:49.700
But then if your car
is, in fact, this far.

00:54:49.700 --> 00:54:54.290
So you need another
set of cables here,

00:54:54.290 --> 00:54:57.980
your loss is going
to go up, [INAUDIBLE]

00:54:57.980 --> 00:55:00.900
just because the
distance is going up.

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:01.590
Yeah.

00:55:01.590 --> 00:55:05.010
AUDIENCE: And the context
of loss [INAUDIBLE]..

00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:07.320
AMY BANZAERT: The loss has
to do with your contacts?

00:55:07.320 --> 00:55:08.158
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:55:08.158 --> 00:55:09.950
Is there a loss there?

00:55:09.950 --> 00:55:11.428
[INAUDIBLE]

00:55:11.428 --> 00:55:12.220
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

00:55:12.220 --> 00:55:13.870
There's going to be
losses everywhere,

00:55:13.870 --> 00:55:17.360
but I would say the length is
going to dominate over that.

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:20.740
So again, this is the
type of engineering

00:55:20.740 --> 00:55:24.280
where you focus on what's going
to dominate and pay attention

00:55:24.280 --> 00:55:24.830
to that.

00:55:24.830 --> 00:55:29.510
And the stuff that is small
you don't even worry about,

00:55:29.510 --> 00:55:31.328
which is most of
engineering, until you get

00:55:31.328 --> 00:55:32.620
into the super-detailed design.

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:36.010
Yeah.

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:37.972
So you worry about it.

00:55:37.972 --> 00:55:39.430
And one of the
things that's really

00:55:39.430 --> 00:55:42.880
interesting on your lanterns
is that a lot of you

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:44.020
didn't use wire.

00:55:44.020 --> 00:55:49.610
You used found materials,
like paper clips and--

00:55:49.610 --> 00:55:52.060
I can't remember the name of--

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:54.940
binder clips and all the
variety of things which

00:55:54.940 --> 00:55:57.400
have decent resistance.

00:55:57.400 --> 00:56:00.940
And yet, you have
an LED that's pretty

00:56:00.940 --> 00:56:05.570
robust to high resistance,
and so it worked fine.

00:56:05.570 --> 00:56:07.900
So that is one of the nice
things to think about,

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:09.740
is yes, you're
getting losses there.

00:56:09.740 --> 00:56:12.070
And if you want to get--

00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:14.170
if you want to
perfectly maximize

00:56:14.170 --> 00:56:16.270
the light output of
your LED, then you

00:56:16.270 --> 00:56:17.480
need to worry about it.

00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:20.110
But if the losses are
relatively small, you're fine.

00:56:20.110 --> 00:56:24.820
So if one of you had made a
lantern that was like somehow

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:27.142
had wires that
were 20 feet long,

00:56:27.142 --> 00:56:28.600
then we're going
to start worrying,

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:30.220
depending on how
small your gauge is

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:32.012
and how high the
resistance is in the line.

00:56:32.012 --> 00:56:34.420
But you've got some
flexibility there.

00:56:37.300 --> 00:56:40.790
If anyone got lost here,
which is understandable,

00:56:40.790 --> 00:56:45.070
because it's confusing, and
want to just come talk to us,

00:56:45.070 --> 00:56:46.990
we can go through it with.

00:56:46.990 --> 00:56:50.110
But it's important
to understand this,

00:56:50.110 --> 00:56:52.345
because it's a useful
calculation to be able to make.

00:56:54.850 --> 00:56:58.820
So just in summary, for
microgrids, some of the--

00:57:04.796 --> 00:57:07.750
some of the benefits
are autonomy.

00:57:07.750 --> 00:57:11.210
So if a community in the
middle of nowhere needs power,

00:57:11.210 --> 00:57:14.080
you don't have to wait
until the government decides

00:57:14.080 --> 00:57:16.360
to deliver that power to you.

00:57:16.360 --> 00:57:18.280
You can just build
it, and you're not

00:57:18.280 --> 00:57:21.970
reliant on the
vagaries of politics.

00:57:21.970 --> 00:57:24.070
You can just build it.

00:57:24.070 --> 00:57:28.630
Compatibility, microgrids
can hypothetically

00:57:28.630 --> 00:57:30.230
attach to the macrogrid.

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:33.580
So that's an
opportunity, and you

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:37.780
can connect with some controls.

00:57:37.780 --> 00:57:41.890
You can connect your wind
turbine and your hydro turbine

00:57:41.890 --> 00:57:44.290
and your solar panels
all to the same grid,

00:57:44.290 --> 00:57:46.600
and you've got some
compatibility and flexibility

00:57:46.600 --> 00:57:49.330
opportunities there, as
well as scalability--

00:57:49.330 --> 00:57:51.700
within the limits of
what we just talked about

00:57:51.700 --> 00:57:54.010
of transmission line length.

00:57:54.010 --> 00:57:56.530
You can scale your microgrid.

00:57:56.530 --> 00:58:00.547
So you can build it small,
and then expand it as needed,

00:58:00.547 --> 00:58:02.380
as long as you're paying
attention to you're

00:58:02.380 --> 00:58:03.460
limiting factors.

00:58:03.460 --> 00:58:05.272
Some of the challenges
of microgrids,

00:58:05.272 --> 00:58:06.730
especially in
developing countries,

00:58:06.730 --> 00:58:09.880
are there's always
going to be maintenance.

00:58:09.880 --> 00:58:12.490
And if it's not
run by a utility,

00:58:12.490 --> 00:58:16.360
then it's a local issue
of the maintenance.

00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:18.280
And so who's going to
do that, and are they

00:58:18.280 --> 00:58:19.810
going to do it well?

00:58:19.810 --> 00:58:24.610
Limiting loads, so
in the US, we have

00:58:24.610 --> 00:58:31.000
our fuse boxes that limit
how much power we can draw.

00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:35.050
You don't always necessarily
have fuse boxes on a microgrid,

00:58:35.050 --> 00:58:37.150
or you don't have ones
at each local house,

00:58:37.150 --> 00:58:38.380
because that's expensive.

00:58:38.380 --> 00:58:41.440
And so people might just start
plugging in stuff willy-nilly,

00:58:41.440 --> 00:58:44.350
and then you take down
your whole microgrid.

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:48.160
And then also just private
versus public sector issues.

00:58:48.160 --> 00:58:51.310
There's both issues of that
one of our community partners

00:58:51.310 --> 00:58:54.700
faces, in Nicaragua, where
they build a microgrid.

00:58:54.700 --> 00:58:57.580
And then for whatever
political reason,

00:58:57.580 --> 00:58:59.800
the government
decides to install

00:58:59.800 --> 00:59:04.060
the public grid,
and all of a sudden,

00:59:04.060 --> 00:59:05.650
all that investment
of the microgrid

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:08.020
goes to waste, unless you
can tie in your microgrid

00:59:08.020 --> 00:59:10.840
to the public grid,
which you rarely can,

00:59:10.840 --> 00:59:15.680
because a variety of political
and technical challenges.

00:59:15.680 --> 00:59:19.270
There's just also issues
that I referred to of,

00:59:19.270 --> 00:59:23.200
if it's locally owned, all
those built-in bureaucracies

00:59:23.200 --> 00:59:25.690
that actually can be
very helpful aren't there

00:59:25.690 --> 00:59:27.890
and figuring out
how to manage that.

00:59:27.890 --> 00:59:30.190
But microgrids do
allow you to put power

00:59:30.190 --> 00:59:33.070
in the middle of places that
would otherwise probably

00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:36.350
never get it, or it's going to
be another century before they

00:59:36.350 --> 00:59:36.850
do.

00:59:36.850 --> 00:59:38.420
So it is pretty valuable.

00:59:38.420 --> 00:59:41.530
So with that, let's
take a 10-minute break,

00:59:41.530 --> 00:59:44.560
and then we'll talk about
batteries and the trip.

00:59:44.560 --> 00:59:47.800
This graph should look familiar,
both because I showed it

00:59:47.800 --> 00:59:50.350
last week, and because you
made a vaguely similar one,

00:59:50.350 --> 00:59:53.440
hopefully, in your
homework for today.

00:59:53.440 --> 01:00:01.420
That batteries are brutal in
terms of the energy solutions

01:00:01.420 --> 01:00:02.560
we're used to.

01:00:02.560 --> 01:00:09.350
And just as a good
rule of thumb,

01:00:09.350 --> 01:00:15.180
batteries on the order of 10
[AUDIO OUT] to 10 [AUDIO OUT]

01:00:15.180 --> 01:00:20.080
to 0 megajoules per kilogram.

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:23.250
And you probably
won't memorize this.

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:34.620
I haven't yet, but there's 0.278
kilowatt hours to 1 mega joule.

01:00:34.620 --> 01:00:38.550
But if you can get that
in your head, it's useful.

01:00:38.550 --> 01:00:41.550
And capacitors, which
are another way that we

01:00:41.550 --> 01:00:47.100
store and have even
worse life, are also

01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:49.800
even worse in terms of use.

01:00:49.800 --> 01:00:53.910
And then springs, which
students often suggest,

01:00:53.910 --> 01:00:55.170
is a great way to store.

01:00:55.170 --> 01:00:58.110
And there are some radios and
wind-up flashlights and things

01:00:58.110 --> 01:01:00.600
like that that that it.

01:01:00.600 --> 01:01:03.900
We're talking an even
further order of magnitude

01:01:03.900 --> 01:01:06.960
of [? badness. ?]
And fossil fuels

01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:12.090
are somewhere on the
order of 10 to the 1.

01:01:12.090 --> 01:01:18.300
So again, this is why batteries
are frustrating to use

01:01:18.300 --> 01:01:20.230
but useful.

01:01:20.230 --> 01:01:24.040
So the other thing that--

01:01:24.040 --> 01:01:26.490
so now, talking about
batteries, there's

01:01:26.490 --> 01:01:29.490
a lot of different chemistries
of batteries, and more

01:01:29.490 --> 01:01:31.020
being developed every day.

01:01:31.020 --> 01:01:35.430
And we are going to
be likely sticking

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:38.640
with established technologies,
because the third dimension

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:41.370
on this graph, which
isn't shown, is cost.

01:01:41.370 --> 01:01:44.820
And emergent technologies
are way off the chart

01:01:44.820 --> 01:01:47.160
compared to, say, a lead-acid.

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:50.220
And lead-acid
batteries, for a lot

01:01:50.220 --> 01:01:51.960
of the systems
we're talking about,

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:54.390
are still really expensive,
just because of our costs.

01:01:54.390 --> 01:01:56.610
We're so constrained on costs.

01:01:56.610 --> 01:02:03.990
So alkalines are common and
are relatively inexpensive.

01:02:03.990 --> 01:02:06.930
So they're a great
technology, but the bummer

01:02:06.930 --> 01:02:09.390
is that they're
not rechargeable.

01:02:09.390 --> 01:02:11.170
And so you're replacing
them constantly,

01:02:11.170 --> 01:02:12.600
which is a huge waste issue.

01:02:12.600 --> 01:02:17.890
So that increases
their effective cost.

01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:18.390
Yeah.

01:02:18.390 --> 01:02:21.870
So one excellent
battery resource,

01:02:21.870 --> 01:02:23.970
just if you're trying
to figure out batteries,

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:26.280
is this battery
primer put together

01:02:26.280 --> 01:02:28.320
by David Wallace,
who's my advisor

01:02:28.320 --> 01:02:32.610
and teaches 209, which is the
senior capstone design class.

01:02:32.610 --> 01:02:38.410
And I stole this summary
page from that document.

01:02:38.410 --> 01:02:40.420
So when you're worrying
about batteries,

01:02:40.420 --> 01:02:44.460
there's a ton of different
things you have to worry about

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:46.270
and some of which we
talked about already.

01:02:46.270 --> 01:02:50.760
So the physical characteristics,
the size, the shape,

01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:52.360
and the weight.

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:56.760
It's really frustrating
when you need to,

01:02:56.760 --> 01:02:58.260
for example, power an LED.

01:02:58.260 --> 01:03:00.750
You noticed I gave you
9-volt batteries instead

01:03:00.750 --> 01:03:02.640
of AA batteries.

01:03:02.640 --> 01:03:04.890
Even the double A's or what
you're more familiar with,

01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:08.670
but double A's don't have
the voltage you need,

01:03:08.670 --> 01:03:11.580
unless you connect
them properly.

01:03:11.580 --> 01:03:15.030
But double A's are a much
nicer form factor than 9 volts.

01:03:15.030 --> 01:03:19.740
But you're limited there, and it
gets more extreme the more you

01:03:19.740 --> 01:03:23.640
go into a variety of batteries.

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:27.900
All the different voltage
issues, we're really

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:29.670
talking very simplistically.

01:03:29.670 --> 01:03:32.760
But when you really get
into batteries, figuring out

01:03:32.760 --> 01:03:37.440
exactly what your peak load
is, how much time you have,

01:03:37.440 --> 01:03:42.700
your current, it all matters,
and it can get complicated.

01:03:42.700 --> 01:03:45.060
But the good thing is there's
really great handbooks

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:46.650
out there that explain it all.

01:03:46.650 --> 01:03:48.210
Batteries are really
well documented.

01:03:48.210 --> 01:03:50.880
If you get a battery spec
sheet, you see all the graphs,

01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:52.690
you can figure out
exactly what you need.

01:03:52.690 --> 01:03:54.750
But you do have to
realize going into it

01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:56.542
that you have to consider
all these things,

01:03:56.542 --> 01:03:58.350
that you can't just
grab a car battery,

01:03:58.350 --> 01:04:01.240
and use it, and expect
it's going to work well.

01:04:01.240 --> 01:04:04.860
So for example, a car battery,
as opposed to a deep cycle

01:04:04.860 --> 01:04:08.310
lead acid battery,
both are lead acid.

01:04:08.310 --> 01:04:11.970
And one of the differences
is that a car battery,

01:04:11.970 --> 01:04:17.130
you need a high current for
a very short burst of time,

01:04:17.130 --> 01:04:20.047
just to get the
starter motor to run.

01:04:20.047 --> 01:04:21.630
And then your car
is set, and then you

01:04:21.630 --> 01:04:24.272
can recharge it, thanks
to your alternator,

01:04:24.272 --> 01:04:25.480
when you're driving your car.

01:04:25.480 --> 01:04:29.400
So the car battery
almost never discharges,

01:04:29.400 --> 01:04:32.433
unless you leave your
lights on all night, bummer.

01:04:32.433 --> 01:04:34.350
And then your battery's
dead, and you probably

01:04:34.350 --> 01:04:37.080
need a new one, if you
do that a few times.

01:04:37.080 --> 01:04:40.420
Whereas, a solar panel
installation or wind power,

01:04:40.420 --> 01:04:43.380
if you think about it, you
don't need a really high current

01:04:43.380 --> 01:04:43.920
quickly.

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:47.200
You need a steady current
for a long period of time.

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:50.880
So a car battery's
chemistry, it's

01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:53.617
not made to provide
that type of power.

01:04:53.617 --> 01:04:55.200
So if you try to a
car battery, you're

01:04:55.200 --> 01:04:57.033
going to kill it really
fast, because you're

01:04:57.033 --> 01:04:58.320
discharging it too much.

01:04:58.320 --> 01:05:00.930
And all batteries
have some lower limit

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:03.850
of where their discharge is, and
you can't discharge any lower.

01:05:03.850 --> 01:05:06.100
So that means, if you're
doing anything complicated,

01:05:06.100 --> 01:05:07.320
you can't just use a battery.

01:05:07.320 --> 01:05:09.750
You also have to use some of
charge control or a discharge

01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:12.000
controller, so that you
never discharge your battery

01:05:12.000 --> 01:05:12.520
too much.

01:05:12.520 --> 01:05:14.770
And you never overcharge it,
and all these subtleties.

01:05:14.770 --> 01:05:19.410
So batteries are really
useful, but there's a lot

01:05:19.410 --> 01:05:20.760
of details to worry about.

01:05:20.760 --> 01:05:24.360
So again, as we're talking about
the duty cycling, the charging,

01:05:24.360 --> 01:05:26.040
and the discharge cycling.

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:27.390
Temperature is another thing.

01:05:27.390 --> 01:05:30.060
We're generally working in
climates that are really hot,

01:05:30.060 --> 01:05:34.350
and batteries are ideally used
in a pretty warm environment

01:05:34.350 --> 01:05:36.540
but not 100 degrees.

01:05:36.540 --> 01:05:39.540
And so they don't
always work well

01:05:39.540 --> 01:05:41.760
in degrees [? F ?] in
places we're working.

01:05:41.760 --> 01:05:44.670
And so you have to
account for that.

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:48.390
Service life, so how long
do they last, and is there

01:05:48.390 --> 01:05:49.920
going to be someone--

01:05:49.920 --> 01:05:53.520
some budget to replace
the battery when they die?

01:05:53.520 --> 01:05:57.030
Safety, we're all
used to alkalines,

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:58.080
which are pretty safe.

01:05:58.080 --> 01:05:59.160
They rarely leak.

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:01.860
But a lot of the
deep-cycle batteries

01:06:01.860 --> 01:06:04.710
can actually be
relatively unsafe.

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:06.180
And you need to
worry about them,

01:06:06.180 --> 01:06:09.540
and all batteries you have
to worry about disposal.

01:06:09.540 --> 01:06:12.270
Environment, maintenance
is a huge issue

01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:14.100
on the cheapest
lead acid batteries,

01:06:14.100 --> 01:06:15.542
which are what is often used.

01:06:15.542 --> 01:06:17.500
And then the cost, both
initial and life cycle.

01:06:17.500 --> 01:06:20.370
So there's a ton of stuff
to consider with batteries.

01:06:20.370 --> 01:06:25.020
And then for just
simply thinking about

01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:27.480
say a lead acid
battery, and how it's

01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:30.540
going to integrate into your
system, and how do you size it?

01:06:30.540 --> 01:06:33.330
So you figure out
what your source is,

01:06:33.330 --> 01:06:36.600
and you have to think about
both your daily average

01:06:36.600 --> 01:06:40.440
as well as your peaks and
lows on a day-to-day basis

01:06:40.440 --> 01:06:41.650
and a seasonal basis.

01:06:41.650 --> 01:06:44.460
So if you think about solar,
it's varying over the season,

01:06:44.460 --> 01:06:47.190
and it's varying over
the course of the day.

01:06:47.190 --> 01:06:51.120
Your daily average of your
load, and that also varies.

01:06:51.120 --> 01:06:54.350
If it's hot, people
might have more fans on.

01:06:54.350 --> 01:06:56.850
If you have a party, and you
keep your lights on for longer,

01:06:56.850 --> 01:06:57.618
what have you.

01:06:57.618 --> 01:07:00.640
How many days of storage?

01:07:00.640 --> 01:07:02.790
So if you have no
days of storage,

01:07:02.790 --> 01:07:06.750
you're just relying on the
sun, and you have a cloudy day,

01:07:06.750 --> 01:07:08.210
it doesn't work.

01:07:08.210 --> 01:07:09.930
Your system doesn't
work that night.

01:07:09.930 --> 01:07:12.420
If you have a few days
of storage, great.

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:16.710
So typically, people put in
three to six days, depending.

01:07:16.710 --> 01:07:18.450
And then you also have a budget.

01:07:18.450 --> 01:07:21.120
So you can develop the
perfect battery plan

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:24.030
for what you need,
and then you realize

01:07:24.030 --> 01:07:26.468
that that's going
to cost you $10,000,

01:07:26.468 --> 01:07:27.510
and you only have $1,000.

01:07:27.510 --> 01:07:28.860
And so then you iterate
and figure out, well,

01:07:28.860 --> 01:07:30.540
what can you put on to pull off?

01:07:30.540 --> 01:07:32.760
The things you're
considering are

01:07:32.760 --> 01:07:35.910
your energy source and
usually for renewables

01:07:35.910 --> 01:07:39.360
that's a DC source.

01:07:39.360 --> 01:07:40.950
And then you have
a charge controller

01:07:40.950 --> 01:07:43.650
to make sure that you
don't harm your battery,

01:07:43.650 --> 01:07:49.020
as you're charging it,
and the charge controller

01:07:49.020 --> 01:07:51.090
has some efficiency.

01:07:51.090 --> 01:07:53.860
The battery can
never get too low.

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:58.860
And then you have an inverter
to go from DC to AC current,

01:07:58.860 --> 01:08:02.610
and that has an efficiency all
the way to get to your AC load.

01:08:02.610 --> 01:08:05.010
In some places-- like,
for example, on boats--

01:08:05.010 --> 01:08:10.080
they just use DC lighting,
and everything is--

01:08:10.080 --> 01:08:11.580
on certain boats,
everything is just

01:08:11.580 --> 01:08:13.590
DC, so you don't have
to use the inverter,

01:08:13.590 --> 01:08:14.970
and that's an option too.

01:08:14.970 --> 01:08:17.220
But a lot of people
want appliances

01:08:17.220 --> 01:08:19.620
that are not DC
appliances, because those

01:08:19.620 --> 01:08:21.359
are a limited set.

01:08:21.359 --> 01:08:26.830
So they're all just
different issues to consider.

01:08:26.830 --> 01:08:31.054
So now, I want to-- are there
any questions on batteries?

01:08:31.054 --> 01:08:34.109
I know this was ridiculously
fast and limited,

01:08:34.109 --> 01:08:37.298
but it's hard to--

01:08:37.298 --> 01:08:39.840
it's more interesting and useful
to talk about the batteries,

01:08:39.840 --> 01:08:41.535
once what you actually need.

01:08:41.535 --> 01:08:43.410
And so that's why I'm
trying to just give you

01:08:43.410 --> 01:08:46.029
the resource of what you
might need to think about

01:08:46.029 --> 01:08:48.439
and where you can go
to think about it.

01:08:48.439 --> 01:08:53.370
There's also a handbook that
David Wallace references.

01:08:53.370 --> 01:08:55.308
That's where he got
all of that stuff.

01:08:55.308 --> 01:08:56.100
That's really good.

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:57.399
That's on my desk today.

01:08:57.399 --> 01:08:59.399
So you can look at it
right now, after class,

01:08:59.399 --> 01:09:00.310
if you're interested.

01:09:00.310 --> 01:09:01.977
But then it's going
back to the library,

01:09:01.977 --> 01:09:04.500
because it's due today.

01:09:04.500 --> 01:09:08.069
But you can check it
out from [? Barker. ?]

01:09:08.069 --> 01:09:09.870
There's lots of resources.

01:09:09.870 --> 01:09:13.740
So are there any questions
on batteries just generally?

01:09:13.740 --> 01:09:14.580
OK.

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:20.340
So now, what I want you to
do is two estimation sets,

01:09:20.340 --> 01:09:23.880
again in groups and
again trying to vary

01:09:23.880 --> 01:09:25.260
who you're working with.

01:09:25.260 --> 01:09:27.660
Because you don't know who
you'll be going on the trip

01:09:27.660 --> 01:09:29.827
with, if you're going on
the trip, and who your team

01:09:29.827 --> 01:09:30.810
will be with post-trip.

01:09:30.810 --> 01:09:33.450
So it's nice to get a sense
of everyone in the class

01:09:33.450 --> 01:09:35.439
and get comfortable
working with them.

01:09:35.439 --> 01:09:40.859
So one is estimating the power
required for typical objects

01:09:40.859 --> 01:09:43.319
that you might encounter
in your daily life,

01:09:43.319 --> 01:09:47.520
and then probably building off
of that estimation, each person

01:09:47.520 --> 01:09:49.200
doing a separate one.

01:09:49.200 --> 01:09:51.420
But you can work together
to help you figure it out,

01:09:51.420 --> 01:09:55.800
how much you think
you consume daily.

01:09:55.800 --> 01:09:59.730
And everyone needs to get how
much energy you consume daily,

01:09:59.730 --> 01:10:03.090
and everyone needs to get
to the personal daily energy

01:10:03.090 --> 01:10:03.900
consumption number.

01:10:03.900 --> 01:10:06.780
You need to get a number.

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:11.205
We can probably take, like, 15
minutes to get to that number,

01:10:11.205 --> 01:10:12.830
because you're going
to-- this is going

01:10:12.830 --> 01:10:14.080
to be related to the homework.

01:10:14.080 --> 01:10:17.042
So everyone needs to
turn in a number to me,

01:10:17.042 --> 01:10:18.500
and I'll have a
sheet going around,

01:10:18.500 --> 01:10:19.700
so everyone can
write down what you

01:10:19.700 --> 01:10:21.408
think your personal
energy consumption is

01:10:21.408 --> 01:10:22.520
on a daily basis.

01:10:22.520 --> 01:10:24.230
And again, I know
your days vary.

01:10:24.230 --> 01:10:27.290
Some days you cook with
an oven, and some days you

01:10:27.290 --> 01:10:31.470
don't, but some random average.

01:10:31.470 --> 01:10:32.555
Any questions on this?

01:10:35.310 --> 01:10:35.822
Juan.

01:10:35.822 --> 01:10:37.530
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
energy consumption,

01:10:37.530 --> 01:10:42.180
say you buy food at a store
for a day, or you don't cook,

01:10:42.180 --> 01:10:43.990
should you kind of
[? estimate ?] I guess

01:10:43.990 --> 01:10:45.990
remind me that they
probably cook it in an oven,

01:10:45.990 --> 01:10:48.238
and it probably took
this many watts?

01:10:48.238 --> 01:10:49.030
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:10:49.030 --> 01:10:49.320
Yeah.

01:10:49.320 --> 01:10:49.820
Yeah.

01:10:49.820 --> 01:10:51.930
So just a general guess.

01:10:51.930 --> 01:10:54.720
This is a challenging
assignment,

01:10:54.720 --> 01:10:58.530
because it's really--
there's so many times

01:10:58.530 --> 01:11:03.270
that you're dealing with energy
that is out of your control.

01:11:03.270 --> 01:11:05.230
The majority of
your life is energy

01:11:05.230 --> 01:11:06.480
that you have no control over.

01:11:06.480 --> 01:11:08.580
This building is
often 85 degrees,

01:11:08.580 --> 01:11:12.210
and that wasn't something you
chose or something I chose.

01:11:12.210 --> 01:11:14.970
But there you are, and what
percentage of this building

01:11:14.970 --> 01:11:16.200
is your fault?

01:11:16.200 --> 01:11:20.730
That's a really strange thing
to think about and calculate,

01:11:20.730 --> 01:11:22.780
so you just do your best.

01:11:22.780 --> 01:11:24.420
The other thing
is you should only

01:11:24.420 --> 01:11:26.100
be talking about
consumable energy.

01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:29.280
So you don't need to worry
about the energy required

01:11:29.280 --> 01:11:32.040
to build your laptop,
just the energy you're

01:11:32.040 --> 01:11:37.360
pulling to power the laptop,
as they're using it that day.

01:11:37.360 --> 01:11:38.350
Does that make sense?

01:11:38.350 --> 01:11:41.250
Any other questions?

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:41.750
OK.

01:11:41.750 --> 01:11:42.560
So get started.

01:11:42.560 --> 01:11:44.794
[? Amin ?] and I are here
if you have questions.

01:11:57.160 --> 01:12:00.700
So I have seen a few orders
of magnitude variation

01:12:00.700 --> 01:12:02.050
in what you guys put.

01:12:02.050 --> 01:12:06.100
So this is actually part of
your homework assignment.

01:12:06.100 --> 01:12:09.640
So definitely, you'll
want to go back and do

01:12:09.640 --> 01:12:11.680
some sanity checking
on your number.

01:12:11.680 --> 01:12:14.500
And then also, just
naturally, there

01:12:14.500 --> 01:12:18.490
will be some refinement when
you do it for your homework.

01:12:18.490 --> 01:12:22.567
But yeah, make sure what
number you just wrote down,

01:12:22.567 --> 01:12:25.150
because you're going to use that
in the homework to compare it

01:12:25.150 --> 01:12:27.455
to the value,
recalculate, et cetera,

01:12:27.455 --> 01:12:29.330
but I'll talk about the
homework in a minute.

01:12:29.330 --> 01:12:32.860
So now, are there any
questions on this?

01:12:32.860 --> 01:12:36.490
All these numbers and
whatever else you used,

01:12:36.490 --> 01:12:39.070
it's relatively easy to google.

01:12:39.070 --> 01:12:41.600
So I wanted you to
first do the estimation.

01:12:41.600 --> 01:12:43.570
So you got to practice
with estimation,

01:12:43.570 --> 01:12:47.770
but now you can just
google it and look it up.

01:12:47.770 --> 01:12:50.470
I recommend when
you google to look

01:12:50.470 --> 01:12:55.240
for at least three corroborating
sources that have different--

01:12:55.240 --> 01:12:57.482
that aren't self-referenced,
before you trust it,

01:12:57.482 --> 01:12:58.690
if you're going to do Google.

01:12:58.690 --> 01:13:01.750
Because as all-knowing
as Wikipedia is,

01:13:01.750 --> 01:13:06.390
it's often off, and
so are other sources.

01:13:06.390 --> 01:13:08.060
So Google's fast.

01:13:08.060 --> 01:13:12.940
So if you're not going to get
a real reference text that's

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:17.110
actually believable, then
definitely get a few sources

01:13:17.110 --> 01:13:19.900
before you rely on it.

01:13:19.900 --> 01:13:20.980
OK.

01:13:20.980 --> 01:13:23.590
So the spring break trip.

01:13:23.590 --> 01:13:26.930
You guys, of course,
had questions about it.

01:13:26.930 --> 01:13:32.260
So we're going to Nicaragua,
which is south of here.

01:13:35.650 --> 01:13:39.010
So spring break is
this week, and we

01:13:39.010 --> 01:13:41.990
try to go Saturday
through Saturday.

01:13:41.990 --> 01:13:43.810
But because of
plane ticket costs,

01:13:43.810 --> 01:13:46.870
sometimes, we travel
on a Friday or Saturday

01:13:46.870 --> 01:13:49.540
or Sunday on both ends.

01:13:49.540 --> 01:13:53.500
So I will put up
shortly a form online

01:13:53.500 --> 01:13:55.060
for you to tell
me like would you

01:13:55.060 --> 01:13:57.670
be available to
travel on a Friday.

01:13:57.670 --> 01:14:00.970
Some of you might have classes
after this class, in which case

01:14:00.970 --> 01:14:01.990
that would be no.

01:14:01.990 --> 01:14:05.770
Some of you don't, and so
students who didn't last year

01:14:05.770 --> 01:14:08.220
just skipped the day of class--

01:14:08.220 --> 01:14:11.320
this lab on the Friday, with
my permission obviously,

01:14:11.320 --> 01:14:13.690
to fly to Nicaragua to
make it more affordable.

01:14:13.690 --> 01:14:15.190
So one of the
questions on here will

01:14:15.190 --> 01:14:20.060
be like what is your flexibility
for arriving and departing?

01:14:20.060 --> 01:14:24.490
So that we can minimize plane
ticket costs for ourselves.

01:14:24.490 --> 01:14:26.830
But those are the
approximate dates.

01:14:26.830 --> 01:14:27.680
The cost for you.

01:14:27.680 --> 01:14:29.800
So for D-Lab trips,
students contribute

01:14:29.800 --> 01:14:33.910
$500 which usually does not
even cover the cost of the plane

01:14:33.910 --> 01:14:38.890
ticket, but allows us to make
it possible for most students

01:14:38.890 --> 01:14:41.290
to travel who want to.

01:14:41.290 --> 01:14:44.200
If $500 is personally
prohibitive for you,

01:14:44.200 --> 01:14:45.910
you can come talk
to me, and we can

01:14:45.910 --> 01:14:50.770
see if we can get you a
scholarship for the trip.

01:14:50.770 --> 01:14:54.430
But otherwise, it really
allows D-Lab to do

01:14:54.430 --> 01:14:56.800
a lot of great stuff to have
your contribution that we

01:14:56.800 --> 01:14:58.780
couldn't do if we
paid everything.

01:14:58.780 --> 01:15:02.020
Also, like $500 is
an amazing bargain

01:15:02.020 --> 01:15:03.800
for the trip you're going on.

01:15:03.800 --> 01:15:06.670
And it's nice to have
you personally contribute

01:15:06.670 --> 01:15:08.650
to what you're doing.

01:15:08.650 --> 01:15:10.810
And then also we don't
cover things like medical.

01:15:10.810 --> 01:15:14.290
You'll need some vaccines,
at MIT Medical probably.

01:15:14.290 --> 01:15:15.380
Those cost a little bit.

01:15:15.380 --> 01:15:17.200
We don't pay for those.

01:15:17.200 --> 01:15:20.800
If you need a passport and/or
a visa, that's something

01:15:20.800 --> 01:15:22.060
you cover on your own.

01:15:22.060 --> 01:15:25.210
Then, when you're in country,
you pay for your own food.

01:15:25.210 --> 01:15:27.910
It works out to usually
a few dollars a day.

01:15:27.910 --> 01:15:30.700
So it's pretty affordable
compared to here.

01:15:30.700 --> 01:15:33.550
And then we usually give gifts
to our community partners,

01:15:33.550 --> 01:15:36.640
and that's something that
comes out of your own pocket.

01:15:36.640 --> 01:15:39.760
They're generally
very modest, both

01:15:39.760 --> 01:15:41.260
because of our own
personal budgets,

01:15:41.260 --> 01:15:45.133
and it's pretty, at least to
some, uncomfortable situations

01:15:45.133 --> 01:15:46.300
that we'll talk about later.

01:15:46.300 --> 01:15:49.030
If you go to this place where
people are earning $2 a day

01:15:49.030 --> 01:15:52.480
and present them with something
that's worth a ton of money,

01:15:52.480 --> 01:15:54.770
it's problematic for
a lot of reasons.

01:15:54.770 --> 01:15:57.670
So it's not prohibitive,
but I'm trying

01:15:57.670 --> 01:15:59.750
to make sure you guys are
clear on all the costs.

01:15:59.750 --> 01:16:01.660
But we make sure
that you get there

01:16:01.660 --> 01:16:05.320
with the plane ticket,
housing, transportation, all

01:16:05.320 --> 01:16:06.665
that stuff is covered.

01:16:06.665 --> 01:16:08.290
Do you have any
questions on the costs?

01:16:11.370 --> 01:16:15.110
And the way you pay this is
you can write a check to D-Lab,

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:15.900
generally.

01:16:15.900 --> 01:16:16.770
It's pretty easy.

01:16:16.770 --> 01:16:19.387
AUDIENCE: If you have a US
passport do you get your visa?

01:16:19.387 --> 01:16:20.970
AMY BANZAERT: You
will not need a visa

01:16:20.970 --> 01:16:22.410
if you have a US passport.

01:16:22.410 --> 01:16:24.900
And many other countries
don't require a visa.

01:16:24.900 --> 01:16:25.890
Some do.

01:16:25.890 --> 01:16:28.500
The cost of the visa,
I think last year,

01:16:28.500 --> 01:16:31.290
was on the order
of like $80 or $90.

01:16:31.290 --> 01:16:32.360
Something like that.

01:16:32.360 --> 01:16:34.270
We can figure it out.

01:16:34.270 --> 01:16:37.650
So things to do now.

01:16:37.650 --> 01:16:40.012
There's a lot of things that
need to be posted tonight.

01:16:40.012 --> 01:16:41.970
So you can't literally
do it right after class,

01:16:41.970 --> 01:16:45.820
but one is it'll be a form
online just to get details,

01:16:45.820 --> 01:16:48.900
if you are hoping
to go on the trip

01:16:48.900 --> 01:16:54.690
that you should fill out
ideally before Friday's class.

01:16:54.690 --> 01:16:56.520
Two is check your
passport to make

01:16:56.520 --> 01:17:00.570
sure it expires more than
six months from March,

01:17:00.570 --> 01:17:03.180
because if it doesn't,
you'll need to renew it.

01:17:03.180 --> 01:17:07.290
Because you're sometimes
barred from traveling,

01:17:07.290 --> 01:17:08.977
if your passport
expires too soon,

01:17:08.977 --> 01:17:11.310
because they don't trust that
you'll be able to get back

01:17:11.310 --> 01:17:12.750
into your home country,
and they don't want

01:17:12.750 --> 01:17:13.990
you stuck in their country.

01:17:13.990 --> 01:17:17.250
So we need to make sure
that it doesn't expire.

01:17:17.250 --> 01:17:18.840
And if it does, if
you process it now,

01:17:18.840 --> 01:17:20.340
it's a whole lot
cheaper than if you

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:23.370
have to do it the night before,
and a lot less stressful,

01:17:23.370 --> 01:17:25.560
which is why to check now.

01:17:25.560 --> 01:17:28.650
You need to make a travel clinic
appointment at MIT Medical,

01:17:28.650 --> 01:17:31.900
or you can go to a different
travel clinic if you prefer.

01:17:31.900 --> 01:17:33.420
But MIT Medical
is easy and their

01:17:33.420 --> 01:17:35.640
experienced for your vaccines.

01:17:35.640 --> 01:17:37.732
There's a variety of
things you may need.

01:17:37.732 --> 01:17:39.690
One thing I would encourage
you to talk to them

01:17:39.690 --> 01:17:44.460
about is whether you want
to do malaria or not.

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:46.620
Malaria meds have
some side effects

01:17:46.620 --> 01:17:48.430
that have bothered
students in the past.

01:17:48.430 --> 01:17:51.360
So some people opt to risk it.

01:17:51.360 --> 01:17:53.670
The risk of malaria
is relatively low,

01:17:53.670 --> 01:17:56.735
in Nicaragua, where we'll
be, but it's non-zero.

01:17:56.735 --> 01:17:58.110
So it's up to you
and your doctor

01:17:58.110 --> 01:18:02.400
to decide where you fall
on that cost-benefit ratio,

01:18:02.400 --> 01:18:04.950
but it is something
to discuss with them

01:18:04.950 --> 01:18:06.900
rather than just
taking it immediately.

01:18:06.900 --> 01:18:08.820
And then make sure they
will, but make sure

01:18:08.820 --> 01:18:13.650
they give you a prescription for
Cipro or a similar antibiotic,

01:18:13.650 --> 01:18:16.320
so that if you do
get sick in country,

01:18:16.320 --> 01:18:19.110
you can take an
antibiotic without having

01:18:19.110 --> 01:18:22.440
to find a clinic that has
it, which can be challenging.

01:18:22.440 --> 01:18:24.600
But that's standard.

01:18:24.600 --> 01:18:28.000
They'll do that at MIT, but
in case you go somewhere else.

01:18:28.000 --> 01:18:32.090
And then the other thing is to
figure out, ideally by Friday,

01:18:32.090 --> 01:18:34.590
because the sooner we buy plane
tickets, the more affordable

01:18:34.590 --> 01:18:35.610
they'll be.

01:18:35.610 --> 01:18:37.470
If you're prepared to
commit to the trip,

01:18:37.470 --> 01:18:39.233
and if you're going
on the trip, we're

01:18:39.233 --> 01:18:41.400
really expecting that you're
committing to the class

01:18:41.400 --> 01:18:41.970
as well.

01:18:41.970 --> 01:18:44.512
Because we're investing a lot
on you to take you on the trip.

01:18:44.512 --> 01:18:46.220
And so while drop date
is after the trip,

01:18:46.220 --> 01:18:48.470
and you could drop it, we're
really expecting that you

01:18:48.470 --> 01:18:49.200
won't.

01:18:49.200 --> 01:18:50.220
So it's on your honor.

01:18:50.220 --> 01:18:52.387
There's nothing I can do,
but you do need to commit.

01:18:52.387 --> 01:18:54.262
The other thing is if
you commit to the trip,

01:18:54.262 --> 01:18:56.580
and we buy your plane tickets,
and then you back out,

01:18:56.580 --> 01:18:59.970
you will be responsible for the
cost of the plane ticket, which

01:18:59.970 --> 01:19:04.840
is on the order of $600.

01:19:04.840 --> 01:19:05.340
Yeah.

01:19:05.340 --> 01:19:07.230
So you do need to
decide, am I doing this?

01:19:07.230 --> 01:19:08.640
Am I not?

01:19:08.640 --> 01:19:11.610
And then I'll post the packing
list again tonight or tomorrow.

01:19:11.610 --> 01:19:13.680
You obviously don't need
to start packing today,

01:19:13.680 --> 01:19:18.150
unless you're incredibly
like my mother,

01:19:18.150 --> 01:19:20.440
packing months in advance.

01:19:20.440 --> 01:19:24.445
But it is helpful to see like,
oh, I don't have any summer

01:19:24.445 --> 01:19:26.820
clothing right now that I'm
willing to bring to Nicaragua

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:27.840
and probably have trash.

01:19:27.840 --> 01:19:30.720
So you can order it now
rather than figuring it out

01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:33.820
the night before to start
thinking about that.

01:19:33.820 --> 01:19:35.790
Are there any other
questions about the trip

01:19:35.790 --> 01:19:36.720
that I can answer?

01:19:36.720 --> 01:19:38.830
AUDIENCE: As far as
like, I mean, obviously

01:19:38.830 --> 01:19:40.830
I'm not packing now, but
for clothing and stuff,

01:19:40.830 --> 01:19:42.000
what's appropriate there?

01:19:42.000 --> 01:19:42.792
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:19:42.792 --> 01:19:45.240
So you want to be
relatively conservative,

01:19:45.240 --> 01:19:48.330
so you don't want to be
wearing like super thin tank

01:19:48.330 --> 01:19:49.260
tops and stuff.

01:19:49.260 --> 01:19:51.070
Sometimes, it's OK
to wear a tank top.

01:19:51.070 --> 01:19:53.730
Oftentimes, you want to have at
least your shoulders covered.

01:19:53.730 --> 01:19:55.897
And then you wouldn't want
to wear any short shorts,

01:19:55.897 --> 01:20:01.020
but stuff that comes down
below your knee and/or skirts

01:20:01.020 --> 01:20:01.590
and pants.

01:20:01.590 --> 01:20:04.500
Nicaragua is pretty
chill about this stuff

01:20:04.500 --> 01:20:06.550
compared to some
places that D-Lab goes.

01:20:06.550 --> 01:20:08.910
The women don't only have
to wear dresses and things

01:20:08.910 --> 01:20:10.952
like that, which is a nice
thing about Nicaragua.

01:20:10.952 --> 01:20:13.950
But we do try to
dress conservatively,

01:20:13.950 --> 01:20:16.583
and one of the things
students questioned

01:20:16.583 --> 01:20:18.000
last year is they
would see people

01:20:18.000 --> 01:20:20.637
on the street dressing
entirely not conservatively,

01:20:20.637 --> 01:20:21.720
and what's going on there?

01:20:21.720 --> 01:20:25.720
And it's because when we're in
more rural areas, number one,

01:20:25.720 --> 01:20:28.295
there's a more
conservative culture.

01:20:28.295 --> 01:20:29.920
And we are going to
pretty rural areas.

01:20:29.920 --> 01:20:33.460
Number two is that we are
representing MIT, the United

01:20:33.460 --> 01:20:35.560
States, et cetera, et cetera.

01:20:35.560 --> 01:20:38.015
And so we don't want to
be on the edge of things

01:20:38.015 --> 01:20:39.390
that people might
find offensive,

01:20:39.390 --> 01:20:41.223
even though some other
people might wear it.

01:20:41.223 --> 01:20:45.270
We want to be looking as
professional and respectful

01:20:45.270 --> 01:20:45.958
as possible.

01:20:45.958 --> 01:20:48.000
So even though you might
see people wearing stuff

01:20:48.000 --> 01:20:49.852
that I specifically
said not to wear,

01:20:49.852 --> 01:20:51.060
there's a reason I said that.

01:20:51.060 --> 01:20:52.840
It's not just
because I'm uptight.

01:20:52.840 --> 01:20:54.750
It's because I want to
make sure that we're

01:20:54.750 --> 01:20:58.920
presenting a respectful face
to our community partners.

01:20:58.920 --> 01:21:00.870
It's really hot in Nicaragua.

01:21:00.870 --> 01:21:03.000
So you definitely
want light clothes,

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:04.350
as light clothes as possible.

01:21:04.350 --> 01:21:07.482
Like we recommend people
bring like a light fleece,

01:21:07.482 --> 01:21:08.940
because it might
get cold at night,

01:21:08.940 --> 01:21:10.600
but oftentimes, it's never used.

01:21:10.600 --> 01:21:12.248
Other times, it is.

01:21:12.248 --> 01:21:14.290
Nicaragua doesn't have a
huge seasonal variation,

01:21:14.290 --> 01:21:17.410
but it still varies a bit.

01:21:17.410 --> 01:21:18.910
You'll definitely
want a swimsuit

01:21:18.910 --> 01:21:21.520
and, again, something on
the more conservative side

01:21:21.520 --> 01:21:26.590
is better than a Brazilian
bikini for this trip.

01:21:26.590 --> 01:21:30.190
Any other questions about that?

01:21:30.190 --> 01:21:33.083
The purpose of the trip, which
I completely skipped over,

01:21:33.083 --> 01:21:34.750
seems like a relevant
thing to bring up.

01:21:37.270 --> 01:21:39.940
The main thrust of this
class is the projects

01:21:39.940 --> 01:21:41.440
that we're going
to be working on.

01:21:41.440 --> 01:21:43.300
And I'll talk more
about them next week.

01:21:43.300 --> 01:21:46.900
But they're projects that are
beneficial to our community

01:21:46.900 --> 01:21:51.303
partners, ideally are beneficial
to a broad scale of people.

01:21:51.303 --> 01:21:52.720
So that we're
working specifically

01:21:52.720 --> 01:21:54.580
with specific
clients, but it's not

01:21:54.580 --> 01:21:56.590
just hypothetically a one-off.

01:21:56.590 --> 01:21:59.960
It could have greater impact.

01:21:59.960 --> 01:22:03.700
And so the idea is that
starting next week, likely,

01:22:03.700 --> 01:22:06.490
you guys will be
working to start

01:22:06.490 --> 01:22:09.550
thinking about those projects
as part of the homework

01:22:09.550 --> 01:22:12.100
and going through the very
initial stages of the design

01:22:12.100 --> 01:22:14.098
process, so that when
you're in country,

01:22:14.098 --> 01:22:15.640
you can talk to them
about, hey, this

01:22:15.640 --> 01:22:17.290
is the idea, what
we're thinking about.

01:22:17.290 --> 01:22:18.512
This is what it'll look like.

01:22:18.512 --> 01:22:19.720
What do you think about this?

01:22:19.720 --> 01:22:20.590
Give me feedback.

01:22:20.590 --> 01:22:24.040
And so that way you can really
get a lot of information

01:22:24.040 --> 01:22:25.270
very quickly.

01:22:25.270 --> 01:22:28.162
That is much, much harder
to get if you don't travel.

01:22:28.162 --> 01:22:29.620
So that's the idea,
as well as just

01:22:29.620 --> 01:22:31.328
to be familiar with
the different country

01:22:31.328 --> 01:22:32.800
and what life is like there.

01:22:32.800 --> 01:22:35.140
It's a pretty great
experience for most people

01:22:35.140 --> 01:22:37.900
that really enriches your
educational experience just

01:22:37.900 --> 01:22:41.290
period, to get to travel
to a developing country

01:22:41.290 --> 01:22:43.930
and see how great
and how challenging

01:22:43.930 --> 01:22:48.020
life can be in a
different place.

01:22:48.020 --> 01:22:49.660
So it's both from a
project standpoint

01:22:49.660 --> 01:22:50.702
and a general standpoint.

01:22:50.702 --> 01:22:51.445
Jess.

01:22:51.445 --> 01:22:53.920
AUDIENCE: Maybe this I can
ask you about after class.

01:22:53.920 --> 01:22:56.800
But could you talk a little bit
about the security and safety

01:22:56.800 --> 01:22:57.898
issues [INAUDIBLE]?

01:22:57.898 --> 01:22:58.690
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:22:58.690 --> 01:23:04.450
So Nicaragua, the people
I know who go down there

01:23:04.450 --> 01:23:06.580
or who live and work
down there consider

01:23:06.580 --> 01:23:09.760
it comparable to
any place in the US

01:23:09.760 --> 01:23:11.900
that's relatively on
the dangerous side.

01:23:11.900 --> 01:23:14.440
So it is no more dangerous
than spending time

01:23:14.440 --> 01:23:17.590
in New York, New York City,
or something like that.

01:23:17.590 --> 01:23:19.000
It's different.

01:23:19.000 --> 01:23:21.580
You probably don't speak
the language as well.

01:23:21.580 --> 01:23:23.060
You're not familiar with things.

01:23:23.060 --> 01:23:24.610
So you have to be
more on your guard

01:23:24.610 --> 01:23:26.260
than you might be in New York.

01:23:26.260 --> 01:23:29.590
But in terms of actual
dangers, it's relatively low.

01:23:29.590 --> 01:23:32.470
There have been some issues
more recently in Managua,

01:23:32.470 --> 01:23:35.500
the capital city, with people--

01:23:35.500 --> 01:23:38.280
kidnapping scams
with taxis, where

01:23:38.280 --> 01:23:40.030
you don't know, where
you get into a taxi,

01:23:40.030 --> 01:23:42.590
and then they mug
you, basically.

01:23:42.590 --> 01:23:47.950
So we don't use taxis where
we don't know the taxi

01:23:47.950 --> 01:23:48.865
driver personally.

01:23:48.865 --> 01:23:50.740
And we have a lot of
connections in Nicaragua

01:23:50.740 --> 01:23:53.900
which allows us to
maintain your safety.

01:23:53.900 --> 01:23:56.350
So what we do is we take
all reasonable precautions.

01:23:56.350 --> 01:23:58.070
Can I guarantee that
nothing will happen?

01:23:58.070 --> 01:23:59.290
Absolutely not.

01:23:59.290 --> 01:24:01.300
Do we help prepare
you to make sure

01:24:01.300 --> 01:24:03.490
that we minimize the chances
of anything happening?

01:24:03.490 --> 01:24:04.180
Definitely.

01:24:04.180 --> 01:24:07.510
And I think chances are
good that you will come back

01:24:07.510 --> 01:24:11.650
with all your belongings and
everything intact, no problem.

01:24:11.650 --> 01:24:13.660
But of course, I
can't guarantee it,

01:24:13.660 --> 01:24:16.390
just as I can't guarantee
you won't get mugged walking

01:24:16.390 --> 01:24:17.890
home to your dorm tonight.

01:24:17.890 --> 01:24:21.350
It's just a reality of life,
but it is relatively safe.

01:24:21.350 --> 01:24:27.168
You can go on the
US Embassy sites--

01:24:27.168 --> 01:24:28.210
I think it's US Embassy--

01:24:28.210 --> 01:24:31.090
US government sites to read up
on what they recommend about

01:24:31.090 --> 01:24:33.790
travel in Nicaragua and what
are the specific dangers,

01:24:33.790 --> 01:24:35.623
if there's something
you're concerned about.

01:24:35.623 --> 01:24:38.560
But I've traveled there
four times last year,

01:24:38.560 --> 01:24:42.160
and I brought
expensive equipment.

01:24:42.160 --> 01:24:44.830
Though I was very good
about only taking it out

01:24:44.830 --> 01:24:46.580
when I was inside
a secure location

01:24:46.580 --> 01:24:48.890
and keeping it very secure,
and nothing disappeared.

01:24:48.890 --> 01:24:49.750
I had no problem.

01:24:49.750 --> 01:24:52.150
I had a student this past
summer who was there,

01:24:52.150 --> 01:24:54.460
and she had $30 stolen out
of her shoe or something

01:24:54.460 --> 01:24:57.190
like that, but that was fine.

01:24:57.190 --> 01:25:01.030
So we've never had something
more significant than that.

01:25:01.030 --> 01:25:04.090
But it is always a risk.

01:25:04.090 --> 01:25:04.960
Yeah.

01:25:04.960 --> 01:25:06.760
AUDIENCE: Where in
Nicaragua are we going?

01:25:06.760 --> 01:25:07.552
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:25:07.552 --> 01:25:10.600
So I'm still figuring that out.

01:25:10.600 --> 01:25:16.390
Last year, we
worked near Ocotal.

01:25:16.390 --> 01:25:20.060
So Managua is the capital
city where we'll fly into.

01:25:20.060 --> 01:25:22.840
And so last year, so
we flew into Managua.

01:25:22.840 --> 01:25:24.820
One group worked up in Ocotal.

01:25:24.820 --> 01:25:28.600
One group worked in Bluefields,
on the Atlantic coast,

01:25:28.600 --> 01:25:33.460
which is a very different
population than the Pacific

01:25:33.460 --> 01:25:34.510
coast.

01:25:34.510 --> 01:25:37.750
The Atlantic coast has a
lot of Caribbean influence,

01:25:37.750 --> 01:25:40.970
as well as Indigenous
populations at a high level.

01:25:40.970 --> 01:25:43.480
And so it's a really
interesting part of the country,

01:25:43.480 --> 01:25:45.100
but it's very
expensive, so we may not

01:25:45.100 --> 01:25:48.220
be able to afford to
go there this year.

01:25:48.220 --> 01:25:51.130
So we worked in
Ocotal and Bluefields

01:25:51.130 --> 01:25:54.790
and in the Matagalpa area.

01:25:54.790 --> 01:25:57.970
We may go to Jinotega this year
as well, and the way it works

01:25:57.970 --> 01:26:02.310
is that you're on a mini team,
and the team goes to one place.

01:26:02.310 --> 01:26:05.830
So last year, one team went
to Bluefields, one place

01:26:05.830 --> 01:26:08.510
to Ocotal, one team to
Matagalpa type of thing.

01:26:08.510 --> 01:26:10.960
So I don't know exactly
where we'll be going.

01:26:10.960 --> 01:26:12.235
It'll be relatively rural.

01:26:15.220 --> 01:26:16.480
Yeah.

01:26:16.480 --> 01:26:19.120
So there's not a great answer.

01:26:19.120 --> 01:26:21.640
It's real time figuring it out
with our community partners

01:26:21.640 --> 01:26:22.340
right now.

01:26:22.340 --> 01:26:24.610
So as I know more,
you'll know more.

01:26:24.610 --> 01:26:28.090
So the Poor People's Energy
Outlook, was it helpful?

01:26:28.090 --> 01:26:32.800
Was it surprising?

01:26:32.800 --> 01:26:35.285
AUDIENCE: I thought the
focus on the opportunity cost

01:26:35.285 --> 01:26:36.270
was really interesting.

01:26:36.270 --> 01:26:40.710
So energies that [INAUDIBLE]
like providing energy

01:26:40.710 --> 01:26:43.680
for themselves, where
energy access is poor

01:26:43.680 --> 01:26:45.960
is just a really big deal.

01:26:45.960 --> 01:26:47.543
And I was actually
pretty surprised

01:26:47.543 --> 01:26:49.710
to hear you say that it was
like a leisure activity.

01:26:49.710 --> 01:26:51.390
[INAUDIBLE]

01:26:51.390 --> 01:26:54.050
AMY BANZAERT: So it's
both a leisure activity

01:26:54.050 --> 01:26:58.730
and a huge hassle.

01:26:58.730 --> 01:26:59.570
It's a combo.

01:26:59.570 --> 01:27:02.420
And so the issue is,
would most people

01:27:02.420 --> 01:27:04.970
prefer not to be carrying
65 pounds of wood

01:27:04.970 --> 01:27:07.100
back and forth for a
few miles every day?

01:27:07.100 --> 01:27:08.780
Of course, absolutely.

01:27:08.780 --> 01:27:10.670
But is there an
aspect of that that's

01:27:10.670 --> 01:27:12.830
important culturally
or personally?

01:27:12.830 --> 01:27:13.400
Yes.

01:27:13.400 --> 01:27:17.180
And so when you're eliminating
that really huge burden,

01:27:17.180 --> 01:27:21.290
you have to consider the
positive aspect of that burden,

01:27:21.290 --> 01:27:24.260
and how do you make sure not
to destroy that positive aspect

01:27:24.260 --> 01:27:26.550
if it's too important
to a community?

01:27:26.550 --> 01:27:30.770
So again, we're in
very gray, realms

01:27:30.770 --> 01:27:33.290
where I did not mean
to say, if I did say,

01:27:33.290 --> 01:27:35.030
that it's a leisure
activity that's fun.

01:27:35.030 --> 01:27:37.670
And everyone would love to carry
65 pounds of wood every day.

01:27:37.670 --> 01:27:39.590
And frankly, why aren't
you guys doing it,

01:27:39.590 --> 01:27:41.300
because I highly
recommend it, and I've

01:27:41.300 --> 01:27:42.633
been starting to do that myself.

01:27:42.633 --> 01:27:43.920
Like of course not.

01:27:43.920 --> 01:27:44.420
[LAUGHTER]

01:27:44.420 --> 01:27:45.257
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:27:45.257 --> 01:27:46.090
AMY BANZAERT: Right.

01:27:46.090 --> 01:27:51.050
However, if there is a reality
that women in developing

01:27:51.050 --> 01:27:52.620
countries tend to
just live at home,

01:27:52.620 --> 01:27:53.870
and they take care of the kid.

01:27:53.870 --> 01:27:56.453
And you take care of the fire,
and you take care of your home.

01:27:56.453 --> 01:27:57.230
And that's it.

01:27:57.230 --> 01:27:59.360
And the only
opportunity you have

01:27:59.360 --> 01:28:05.250
to socialize and get a little
sanity time is through work.

01:28:05.250 --> 01:28:09.000
And so if you eliminate
that work, hooray.

01:28:09.000 --> 01:28:11.270
But if you don't then
build in some other way

01:28:11.270 --> 01:28:13.700
to get that sanity
time, then maybe people

01:28:13.700 --> 01:28:15.740
won't be willing to take
that work elimination,

01:28:15.740 --> 01:28:18.310
depending on the cost benefits.

01:28:18.310 --> 01:28:19.360
Yeah.

01:28:19.360 --> 01:28:24.670
AUDIENCE: Is that
like [INAUDIBLE]

01:28:24.670 --> 01:28:28.120
because I feel like that's
how we got where we are today.

01:28:28.120 --> 01:28:31.900
We don't [INAUDIBLE] around
65 pounds of water, but we

01:28:31.900 --> 01:28:33.250
[INAUDIBLE].

01:28:33.250 --> 01:28:36.880
But we do have other
hobbies or have other things

01:28:36.880 --> 01:28:38.470
that have occupied that time.

01:28:38.470 --> 01:28:40.360
AMY BANZAERT: Well,
the issue is not--

01:28:40.360 --> 01:28:40.630
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:28:40.630 --> 01:28:42.250
AUDIENCE: It doesn't seem to
come up in our reading, so.

01:28:42.250 --> 01:28:42.970
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:28:42.970 --> 01:28:44.262
It didn't come to the readings.

01:28:44.262 --> 01:28:47.110
So there is no
reading, and there

01:28:47.110 --> 01:28:50.740
is no person who's the expert
on all of this infinitely

01:28:50.740 --> 01:28:52.190
and can give you every insight.

01:28:52.190 --> 01:28:54.430
That's why I'm going to give
you a range of readings,

01:28:54.430 --> 01:28:55.610
and you're going to
hear my perspective.

01:28:55.610 --> 01:28:57.027
But I'm also giving
your readings,

01:28:57.027 --> 01:28:58.960
so it's not just my perspective.

01:28:58.960 --> 01:29:03.633
The issue is not ensuring
leisure time necessarily.

01:29:03.633 --> 01:29:05.050
That's not what
I'm trying to say.

01:29:05.050 --> 01:29:07.930
What I'm trying to
say is that if there's

01:29:07.930 --> 01:29:09.940
something about this
activity, that it's

01:29:09.940 --> 01:29:12.340
a really sucky
activity, but there's

01:29:12.340 --> 01:29:14.590
this really important
aspect to it

01:29:14.590 --> 01:29:17.170
that people really value,
if you provide them

01:29:17.170 --> 01:29:21.220
an alternative to that sucky
activity that doesn't address

01:29:21.220 --> 01:29:24.640
the part that's important to
them, they may not adopt it,

01:29:24.640 --> 01:29:26.950
regardless of how hard you work.

01:29:26.950 --> 01:29:29.800
So I don't know if
I mentioned this,

01:29:29.800 --> 01:29:33.280
but there's the classic
example of latrines.

01:29:33.280 --> 01:29:35.050
And you provide
people with latrines,

01:29:35.050 --> 01:29:37.360
and you provide the
community with the latrine,

01:29:37.360 --> 01:29:39.040
and they never use it.

01:29:39.040 --> 01:29:41.530
[INAUDIBLE] They use
it for a chicken coop.

01:29:41.530 --> 01:29:42.815
They use it to store grain.

01:29:42.815 --> 01:29:43.690
They use it for this.

01:29:43.690 --> 01:29:44.565
They use it for that.

01:29:44.565 --> 01:29:46.900
But they don't use it
for the actual purpose

01:29:46.900 --> 01:29:48.910
that a latrine serves.

01:29:48.910 --> 01:29:51.310
And it blows people's minds.

01:29:51.310 --> 01:29:52.510
I installed this latrine.

01:29:52.510 --> 01:29:53.320
You guys helped me.

01:29:53.320 --> 01:29:54.310
We did it all right.

01:29:54.310 --> 01:29:55.480
And why aren't you using it?

01:29:55.480 --> 01:29:57.070
And it's usually
something subtle

01:29:57.070 --> 01:29:59.230
like the latrine is
the only place where

01:29:59.230 --> 01:30:02.182
there's a door that locks, and
that's the only secure place.

01:30:02.182 --> 01:30:04.390
And it's more important for
me to have a secure place

01:30:04.390 --> 01:30:06.590
than it is for me to
have a hygienic place.

01:30:06.590 --> 01:30:08.680
And so therefore, I'm not
going to use the latrine

01:30:08.680 --> 01:30:10.790
for the intended purpose.

01:30:10.790 --> 01:30:13.483
So there's all these unintended
consequences of any technology

01:30:13.483 --> 01:30:15.400
that you deliver, and
you have to really think

01:30:15.400 --> 01:30:18.070
about those unintended
consequences,

01:30:18.070 --> 01:30:19.800
or unanticipated
consequences, if you're

01:30:19.800 --> 01:30:21.550
going to deliver
something that's actually

01:30:21.550 --> 01:30:25.210
going to be used in the way that
you are hoping it will be used.

01:30:25.210 --> 01:30:28.940
Does that help provide
clarity to that subtlety?

01:30:28.940 --> 01:30:29.440
Yeah.

01:30:29.440 --> 01:30:32.590
So it's really hard to
wrap your head around this,

01:30:32.590 --> 01:30:33.995
because there's no--

01:30:33.995 --> 01:30:35.620
there's an infinite
number of examples,

01:30:35.620 --> 01:30:39.130
but it's hard to make a really
clean, generalized statement,

01:30:39.130 --> 01:30:41.890
because it's all subtle.

01:30:41.890 --> 01:30:47.380
One thing I thought was
interesting about the energy

01:30:47.380 --> 01:30:50.800
outlook was the
Energy Access Index,

01:30:50.800 --> 01:30:56.860
and how they rated everything,
and whether that's actually

01:30:56.860 --> 01:31:00.910
the right way to
approach things.

01:31:00.910 --> 01:31:02.893
It's really useful to
have an access index

01:31:02.893 --> 01:31:04.810
and to be able to compare
where people are at,

01:31:04.810 --> 01:31:09.160
but are these different
steps on each level

01:31:09.160 --> 01:31:11.470
actually the right steps?

01:31:11.470 --> 01:31:14.570
And that's open to debate.

01:31:14.570 --> 01:31:17.200
I'm not asking for a
yes, it is; no, it's not.

01:31:17.200 --> 01:31:18.130
I don't know myself.

01:31:18.130 --> 01:31:23.260
But it is interesting.

01:31:23.260 --> 01:31:27.280
Do you actually prefer
having low-voltage DC access

01:31:27.280 --> 01:31:30.970
that you control to a
240-volt connection that's

01:31:30.970 --> 01:31:32.380
poor quality and intermittent?

01:31:32.380 --> 01:31:36.670
I'd probably prefer step
three than step four.

01:31:36.670 --> 01:31:39.220
So I think it is an
interesting thing

01:31:39.220 --> 01:31:41.560
to read these
documents critically,

01:31:41.560 --> 01:31:44.022
that even though this is
like practical action,

01:31:44.022 --> 01:31:46.480
and they're an amazing group,
and they're really thoughtful

01:31:46.480 --> 01:31:51.080
and love their work, this
is still real, hard stuff.

01:31:51.080 --> 01:31:52.580
And so it can be subtle.

01:31:52.580 --> 01:31:57.270
Were there any other
thoughts people noticed?

01:31:57.270 --> 01:31:58.380
Yeah.

01:31:58.380 --> 01:32:00.172
AUDIENCE: I wanted to
see more comparisons.

01:32:00.172 --> 01:32:02.298
When it was talking about
the [INAUDIBLE] victims,

01:32:02.298 --> 01:32:03.840
I wanted to know
what that number was

01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:05.507
for the [? States ?]
[INAUDIBLE] I know.

01:32:05.507 --> 01:32:10.470
And I was like, well, yes,
60% of people, [INAUDIBLE]..

01:32:10.470 --> 01:32:14.560
And I'm like, well, I feel
like that's actualluy, I mean,

01:32:14.560 --> 01:32:17.310
[INAUDIBLE] cooking, because
how else do we really

01:32:17.310 --> 01:32:19.708
burn ourselves?

01:32:19.708 --> 01:32:20.500
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah.

01:32:20.500 --> 01:32:21.330
AUDIENCE: Didn't have much
to help me [INAUDIBLE]

01:32:21.330 --> 01:32:21.968
the numbers.

01:32:21.968 --> 01:32:23.010
AMY BANZAERT: Yeah, yeah.

01:32:23.010 --> 01:32:24.990
And yeah, and some
other subtleties

01:32:24.990 --> 01:32:28.230
are how easy is it for
you to get medical care,

01:32:28.230 --> 01:32:29.870
and what are the
implications for you

01:32:29.870 --> 01:32:33.630
if you incapacitate
your hand versus if you

01:32:33.630 --> 01:32:37.050
rely on your hands to feed
your family every day?

01:32:37.050 --> 01:32:38.550
So there's other
subtleties that are

01:32:38.550 --> 01:32:42.360
hard to see from just
a 60% number and even

01:32:42.360 --> 01:32:44.400
comparing to the US.

01:32:44.400 --> 01:32:47.400
But I will also try to
post some resources that

01:32:47.400 --> 01:32:51.330
allow you to compare countries
really easily, so that you can,

01:32:51.330 --> 01:32:53.280
if the documentation
doesn't provide it,

01:32:53.280 --> 01:32:59.790
you can pretty easily look up
on your own, the comparison

01:32:59.790 --> 01:33:01.950
if that's of interest.

01:33:01.950 --> 01:33:02.460
OK.

01:33:02.460 --> 01:33:07.150
So we're fast approaching
the end of the class.

01:33:07.150 --> 01:33:11.520
So this week, the primary
homework assignment

01:33:11.520 --> 01:33:15.030
is what we call the Personal
Energy Consumption Challenge.

01:33:15.030 --> 01:33:17.460
You may or may not know
that in D-Lab development,

01:33:17.460 --> 01:33:22.410
students have to spend
a week on $2 a day.

01:33:22.410 --> 01:33:25.510
And it's challenging,
and it's really helpful.

01:33:25.510 --> 01:33:27.300
And so we were
like, great, we're

01:33:27.300 --> 01:33:29.220
going to make a similar
energy assignment,

01:33:29.220 --> 01:33:32.760
but it's much more complicated
to limit your energy than it

01:33:32.760 --> 01:33:37.440
is to limit your food,
your financial spending.

01:33:37.440 --> 01:33:39.840
Obviously, for some of the
reasons we just talked about,

01:33:39.840 --> 01:33:41.880
that you can't just
walk into a classroom

01:33:41.880 --> 01:33:43.380
and be like, I'm
sorry, professor,

01:33:43.380 --> 01:33:44.880
you need to turn
off that projector,

01:33:44.880 --> 01:33:48.180
and I need the lights
off and the heat off.

01:33:48.180 --> 01:33:50.100
And I can't do my
problem sets today,

01:33:50.100 --> 01:33:52.440
because I can't use light,
and I can't use my computer,

01:33:52.440 --> 01:33:56.070
because my other
class said that.

01:33:56.070 --> 01:34:00.480
We don't actually want you
to fail for a given week

01:34:00.480 --> 01:34:02.400
or make everyone in
your world hate you,

01:34:02.400 --> 01:34:03.790
because you're restricting.

01:34:03.790 --> 01:34:05.910
It's much easier to control
your personal spending

01:34:05.910 --> 01:34:07.500
than your personal energy.

01:34:07.500 --> 01:34:10.705
So the way we did
this is we split it up

01:34:10.705 --> 01:34:12.330
into a few things--
and the other thing

01:34:12.330 --> 01:34:14.982
is that most people have a
budget or a general sense

01:34:14.982 --> 01:34:17.440
of how much you're spending on
a given day or a given week.

01:34:17.440 --> 01:34:19.440
And very few of us as
we just figured out

01:34:19.440 --> 01:34:22.110
have a sense of how much
energy we're spending,

01:34:22.110 --> 01:34:24.760
consuming on a
given day or week.

01:34:24.760 --> 01:34:29.350
So what I would like you to do
is choose two days this week.

01:34:29.350 --> 01:34:31.980
And on the first
day, actually spend

01:34:31.980 --> 01:34:36.430
your day documenting
at all times,

01:34:36.430 --> 01:34:38.250
I'm using my laptop
for this much time.

01:34:38.250 --> 01:34:40.063
I'm in a lit room
for this much time.

01:34:40.063 --> 01:34:41.730
I'm in a heated room
for this much time.

01:34:41.730 --> 01:34:45.390
I'm driving my car for this much
time, all this stuff, and run

01:34:45.390 --> 01:34:49.650
through the numbers, so that you
can get a more refined number

01:34:49.650 --> 01:34:53.460
than your initial estimate.

01:34:53.460 --> 01:34:56.830
I don't want you to spend
1,000 hours on this assignment.

01:34:56.830 --> 01:35:00.390
So it would be very easy
to get so anal that you

01:35:00.390 --> 01:35:03.118
would have to spend a
ridiculous number of hours

01:35:03.118 --> 01:35:03.910
on this assignment.

01:35:03.910 --> 01:35:08.460
And so you need to balance
how refined your number is

01:35:08.460 --> 01:35:11.970
versus you have a life,
and you can't only

01:35:11.970 --> 01:35:14.910
spend it on my class, as
much as I think you should.

01:35:14.910 --> 01:35:18.000
So yeah, so take
that into account.

01:35:18.000 --> 01:35:20.430
One student last
year I think spent

01:35:20.430 --> 01:35:21.840
like 80 hours on
this assignment,

01:35:21.840 --> 01:35:25.120
and I really don't want that.

01:35:25.120 --> 01:35:28.730
So yeah, and then some of the
things you should think about

01:35:28.730 --> 01:35:31.230
is what is in your control, and
what is not in your control,

01:35:31.230 --> 01:35:34.600
and what's sort of
in your control?

01:35:34.600 --> 01:35:38.580
And then on the second
day, and if it's possible,

01:35:38.580 --> 01:35:40.950
and I know there's,
within reason, if you

01:35:40.950 --> 01:35:42.250
can make the two days similar.

01:35:42.250 --> 01:35:44.125
So if one day is a
weekend, and your weekends

01:35:44.125 --> 01:35:46.500
are totally different
from your weekdays,

01:35:46.500 --> 01:35:49.410
if you can try not to
do that, so that you

01:35:49.410 --> 01:35:55.140
can have a comparison
that's vaguely legitimate.

01:35:55.140 --> 01:35:58.650
Try to implement cut-backs
as much as possible

01:35:58.650 --> 01:36:01.440
to cut back significantly.

01:36:01.440 --> 01:36:04.650
Last year, one student cut
back by taking shorter showers.

01:36:04.650 --> 01:36:06.990
That was the entire
cut that they made,

01:36:06.990 --> 01:36:09.310
and that's insufficient.

01:36:09.310 --> 01:36:10.980
So try to really make it hurt.

01:36:10.980 --> 01:36:13.830
Like it hurts to spend
$2 a day for a week.

01:36:13.830 --> 01:36:17.290
But at the same time, try
not to fail your classes.

01:36:17.290 --> 01:36:21.360
So there's a
balancing act there.

01:36:21.360 --> 01:36:23.700
And then document how
hard was it to cut back?

01:36:23.700 --> 01:36:24.870
Where could you cut back?

01:36:24.870 --> 01:36:26.260
What was out of your control?

01:36:26.260 --> 01:36:28.470
And then think about,
but you don't actually

01:36:28.470 --> 01:36:31.530
have to experience,
because it would

01:36:31.530 --> 01:36:35.140
be very challenging and
prohibitive, is what if I said,

01:36:35.140 --> 01:36:39.570
you have to use
10% of the energy

01:36:39.570 --> 01:36:40.770
that you're currently using?

01:36:40.770 --> 01:36:43.950
What would you do if that
was just a mandatory thing?

01:36:43.950 --> 01:36:45.090
How would you live?

01:36:45.090 --> 01:36:47.208
And then run through that
as a thought experiment,

01:36:47.208 --> 01:36:48.750
but you don't actually
have to do it,

01:36:48.750 --> 01:36:52.560
because it would be challenging.

01:36:52.560 --> 01:36:56.370
And the reason for the 10% is
looking at the energy usage

01:36:56.370 --> 01:37:01.980
in the US versus in Nicaragua.

01:37:01.980 --> 01:37:04.480
And then the other assignments
I just want to remind you of.

